<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>2. Introduction</h1>

<h2>2.1 Purpose</h2>
<p>This document specifies the complete set of functional and non-functional requirements for <b>Swarn</b>, an autonomous machine-learning engineering agent platform. It is written to be the single reference that engineers build against, that QA writes test cases from, and that management uses to judge scope and progress.</p>
<p>In plain terms: Swarn is a system you give an ML problem to in ordinary English &mdash; "predict which customers will churn, optimise for AUC" &mdash; and it writes the code, runs it, reads its own results, fixes what broke, tries better ideas, and hands back a working model plus a report of how it got there. It does this without a human driving each step.</p>

<h2>2.2 Scope</h2>
<h3>2.2.1 In scope</h3>
<ul>
<li>An autonomous agent loop (ReAct: reason &rarr; act &rarr; observe) that plans and executes tasks using a registry of 45 built-in tools.</li>
<li>A solution-tree search engine that generates multiple candidate ML solutions in parallel, executes them, scores them, debugs the broken ones and improves the winning ones within an explicit budget.</li>
<li>A sandboxed execution environment (Docker where available, subprocess otherwise) so generated code cannot damage the host.</li>
<li>An end-to-end ML pipeline toolset: data ingestion and validation, feature engineering, multi-candidate model training with hyperparameter optimisation, evaluation and plotting, and deployment packaging.</li>
<li>Cross-run learning: a bounded "playbook" of generalisable lessons plus a searchable archive of past runs, injected into future runs as prior art.</li>
<li>Multi-agent orchestration (Planner &rarr; Coder &rarr; Reviewer &rarr; Tester) with per-role tool restrictions.</li>
<li>Model Context Protocol (MCP) support in both directions: Swarn consumes external MCP servers, and Swarn exposes itself as an MCP server to clients such as Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf and Zed.</li>
<li>Three user interfaces: an interactive REPL, a Typer-based CLI, and a FastAPI web dashboard with live run streaming.</li>
<li>Prompt-injection guardrails and OpenTelemetry tracing.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.2.2 Out of scope</h3>
<ul>
<li>Multi-tenancy, user accounts, and role-based access control. The system currently has no concept of a user identity.</li>
<li>Hosted / SaaS operation. The delivered system is a locally-run developer tool.</li>
<li>Serving trained models in production. Swarn <i>generates</i> a deployable FastAPI service package; it does not host or operate it.</li>
<li>Training or hosting the underlying large language model. Swarn calls one external OpenAI-compatible endpoint.</li>
<li>Data governance, PII detection, and regulated-data handling. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; confirm with the manager whether any regulated data will ever be processed; if yes this becomes an in-scope requirement, not an exclusion.</span></li>
</ul>

<h3>2.2.3 Business objective</h3>
<p>The stated goal in the design material is to build a from-scratch competitor to HeyNeo (a commercial autonomous ML engineer) and to outperform it on the axes that decide benchmark results and real work: parallel solution search, cross-run self-improvement, transparent budgets, and resumability. The reference benchmark cited is MLE-bench, where HeyNeo claims 34.2%.</p>
<p class="note"><b>Assumption to validate.</b> No target benchmark score for Swarn is stated anywhere in the design material. "Beat HeyNeo" is a direction, not an acceptance criterion. <span class="assume">[Assumption: the v1 acceptance target is &ge;34.2% on MLE-bench Lite under an equivalent token budget.]</span> This number is a placeholder and must be agreed before it is used to judge the project.</p>

<h2>2.3 Definitions</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:24%">Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
<tr><td>Agent loop</td><td>The repeating cycle in which the system asks the language model what to do, executes the requested tool, feeds the result back, and repeats until the model signals completion.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Tool</td><td>A Python function registered in a central registry that the language model is allowed to call by name, with a declared JSON input schema.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tool registry</td><td>The single dictionary holding every callable tool. The architectural rule of the project is that this registry grows; the loop that drives it does not change.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Node</td><td>One attempted solution in the search tree: its plan, its code, its execution output, its reviewed metric and its buggy/good verdict.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Journal</td><td>The persisted tree of all nodes for one search run. Saved after every step so a killed run can be resumed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Draft / Debug / Improve</td><td>The three actions the search policy can take: create a new independent solution, fix a broken one, or make one atomic improvement to a working one.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Static gate</td><td>A pre-execution check that rejects code with syntax errors, missing metric prints, or guaranteed hangs, so a sandbox run is not wasted on it.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Playbook</td><td>A hard-capped markdown file of generalisable lessons distilled from finished runs and injected into future runs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Doom loop</td><td>The failure mode where the agent repeats the same tool call and gets the same result indefinitely.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Blackboard</td><td>The small structured state object the multi-agent orchestrator uses to pass outcomes between roles, instead of sharing one chat history.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Guardrail</td><td>A scan of tool output for prompt-injection patterns, which annotates rather than removes the suspicious content.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Artifact (model)</td><td>A trained model held in the in-memory registry together with its metrics, feature columns, target column and held-out test split.</td></tr>
</table>

