<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>3. Overall Description</h1>

<h2>3.1 Product Perspective</h2>
<p>Swarn is a self-contained, locally-installed Python application. It is not a component of a larger product and it does not replace an existing system. It sits between a human engineer and three external dependencies: a deployed language-model endpoint, a container runtime, and optionally a set of external MCP servers.</p>

<pre>
                       ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
   Human engineer ───► │  Interfaces: REPL │ CLI │ Web dashboard   │
                       └───────────────┬──────────────────────────┘
                                       │
                       ┌───────────────▼──────────────────────────┐
                       │  Orchestration: ReAct loop │ Multi-agent  │
                       │                 │ Tree-search engine      │
                       └───────────────┬──────────────────────────┘
                                       │
                       ┌───────────────▼──────────────────────────┐
                       │  Tool registry (45 tools + dynamic MCP)   │
                       └───┬───────────────┬───────────────┬──────┘
                           │               │               │
                  ┌────────▼──────┐ ┌──────▼──────┐ ┌──────▼────────┐
                  │ Execution     │ │ ML pipeline │ │ Knowledge &   │
                  │ sandbox       │ │ (data→deploy)│ │ memory        │
                  └───────────────┘ └─────────────┘ └───────────────┘
                           │                                │
                  ┌────────▼───────┐              ┌─────────▼────────┐
                  │ Docker /       │              │ SQLite FTS5,     │
                  │ subprocess     │              │ ChromaDB, JSON   │
                  └────────────────┘              └──────────────────┘

   External: deployed LLM endpoint (OpenAI-compatible) · external MCP servers
</pre>

<p>The single most important architectural constraint, established at the outset and never violated, is stated in R-4 as: <i>the tool registry is the only thing that grows; the loop that drives it does not change.</i> Every capability added across sixteen development phases either added tools to the registry or added optional configuration to the existing loop. No phase required rewriting the loop's control flow.</p>

<h2>3.2 Product Functions</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:8%">Ref</th><th style="width:30%">Function</th><th>Summary</th></tr>
<tr><td>PF-1</td><td>Autonomous task execution</td><td>Accept a natural-language task and complete it by iteratively selecting and running tools.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>PF-2</td><td>Solution-tree search</td><td>Generate, run, score, debug and improve multiple complete ML solutions within a budget.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PF-3</td><td>Safe code execution</td><td>Run generated Python and shell commands in an isolated sandbox with enforced timeouts and output caps.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>PF-4</td><td>Codebase and document understanding</td><td>Index and semantically search source code, PDFs, images and audio.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PF-5</td><td>ML pipeline automation</td><td>Ingest, validate, engineer features, train, tune, evaluate and package models.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>PF-6</td><td>Self-correction</td><td>Classify tool failures, inject targeted guidance, and abort on unbroken error streaks.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PF-7</td><td>Cross-run learning</td><td>Distil lessons from finished runs into a bounded playbook and a searchable run archive.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>PF-8</td><td>Multi-agent review</td><td>Split work across Planner, Coder, Reviewer and Tester roles with restricted toolsets.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PF-9</td><td>Interoperability</td><td>Consume any MCP server's tools; expose Swarn's own capabilities over MCP.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>PF-10</td><td>Transparency</td><td>Persist a full structured trace of every run; stream steps live; emit OpenTelemetry spans.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PF-11</td><td>Budget control</td><td>Enforce token, wall-clock, step and iteration budgets, and stop cleanly when exceeded.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>PF-12</td><td>Resumability</td><td>Continue a killed search run from its crash-safe journal.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.3 User Classes</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">User class</th><th style="width:26%">Characteristics</th><th style="width:28%">Primary functions used</th><th>Privilege</th></tr>
<tr><td>ML Engineer / Data Scientist</td><td>Technical; the main intended operator. Comfortable with a terminal.</td><td>PF-1, PF-2, PF-5, PF-11, PF-12</td><td>Full. No restriction exists in the system.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Software Engineer</td><td>Uses Swarn as a coding agent rather than an ML agent.</td><td>PF-1, PF-3, PF-4, PF-8</td><td>Full.</td></tr>
<tr><td>MCP Client (machine actor)</td><td>An IDE or agent such as Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf or Zed, connecting over stdio.</td><td>PF-1, PF-2 via four MCP tools</td><td>Full, unauthenticated. See &sect;11.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Reviewer / Manager</td><td>Non-operator. Reads results.</td><td>PF-10 (dashboard, reports, session history)</td><td>Full read <i>and write</i> &mdash; the dashboard has no read-only mode. See RISK-03.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Platform Operator</td><td>Configures the endpoint, sandbox backend and budgets.</td><td>Environment configuration</td><td>Full.</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="note"><b>Observation for the manager.</b> These are usage patterns, not enforced roles. The system implements no authentication and therefore cannot distinguish one user class from another at runtime. Every row above has identical privilege in the delivered build. This is acceptable for a local developer tool and unacceptable for anything shared &mdash; see &sect;11 and RISK-03.</p>

<h2>3.4 Operating Environment</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Element</th><th style="width:32%">Requirement</th><th>Source / note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Language runtime</td><td>Python 3.11 or later</td><td>Sandbox image is <span class="m">python:3.11-slim</span>; codebase uses 3.10+ syntax.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Operating system</td><td>Linux, macOS, Windows</td><td>Cross-platform is an explicit design goal; the subprocess backend uses <span class="m">sys.executable</span> specifically to be Windows-safe.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Container runtime</td><td>Docker (optional)</td><td>Auto-detected via a daemon ping. Absent &rarr; automatic fallback to the subprocess backend.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Language model</td><td>One OpenAI-compatible HTTP endpoint</td><td>Test deployment: Qwen 3.5 9B on Modal. Production endpoint: <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not yet designated.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Network</td><td>Outbound HTTPS to the model endpoint; outbound to huggingface.co on first index/fine-tune</td><td>Offline operation degrades gracefully with an explanatory error string, verified in R-4.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>GPU</td><td>CUDA GPU required only for QLoRA fine-tuning</td><td>All other functions are CPU-only.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storage</td><td>Local filesystem, writable</td><td>Used for <span class="m">runs/</span>, <span class="m">sessions/</span>, <span class="m">knowledge/</span>, <span class="m">.chroma/</span>, <span class="m">workspace/</span>. Sizing: <span class="todo">TODO</span>.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Browser</td><td>Any modern browser with WebSocket support</td><td>Dashboard is a single self-contained HTML page, no build step.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.5 Design Constraints</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:10%">ID</th><th style="width:34%">Constraint</th><th>Rationale / consequence</th></tr>
<tr><td>DC-01</td><td>The ReAct loop's control flow must not be modified to add capability.</td><td>The project's central architectural rule. New capability arrives as new registry entries or optional constructor parameters.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DC-02</td><td>Tools must return errors as strings and must never raise into the loop.</td><td>Lets one self-correction mechanism work uniformly across all 45 tools without per-tool glue. Violating it silently breaks error detection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>DC-03</td><td>All LLM traffic routes to a single deployed OpenAI-compatible endpoint.</td><td>BYO-LLM was deliberately removed. Provider-prefixed model specs are accepted but ignored for routing, except <span class="m">mock:*</span>.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DC-04</td><td>No external message broker may be introduced.</td><td>Stated design position in R-4. It is the direct cause of the cross-process live-streaming limitation (&sect;10.5).</td></tr>
<tr><td>DC-05</td><td>The core test suite must run with zero third-party dependencies and no network access.</td><td><span class="m">python tests/run_tests.py</span> runs 58 tests across 9 modules offline using a scripted mock LLM client.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DC-06</td><td>Heavy or optional dependencies must be imported lazily and behind a cheap safety check.</td><td>Established after a broken PyTorch install crashed evaluation calls unrelated to PyTorch.</td></tr>
<tr><td>DC-07</td><td>Agent file I/O is confined to the workspace directory by an explicit path guard.</td><td>Path traversal outside <span class="m">workspace/</span> is rejected.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DC-08</td><td>MCP client transport is stdio only.</td><td>SSE/HTTP transports are not wired up. Documented limitation in R-4.</td></tr>
<tr><td>DC-09</td><td>Generated code must be compiled before being trusted.</td><td>Derived from a real defect: a template indentation bug produced invalid Python that was invisible on inspection.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.6 Assumptions</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:10%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Assumption</th><th>If false</th></tr>
<tr><td>AS-01</td><td>The deployed model endpoint is reachable and reasonably available during a run.</td><td>Runs fail after 5 retries with an <span class="m">LLMError</span>. There is no offline degraded mode outside tests.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>AS-02</td><td>The operator trusts the task they submit and the data they point at.</td><td>The agent can run arbitrary shell commands. There is no policy layer between intent and execution.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AS-03</td><td>Swarn runs on a trusted single-user machine or a private network segment.</td><td>Every unauthenticated surface in &sect;11 becomes an immediate exposure.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>AS-04</td><td>Datasets fit in the memory of the host machine.</td><td>The dataset registry is an in-process dictionary of pandas DataFrames. No spill-to-disk exists. <span class="assume">[Assumption: datasets up to ~2 GB.]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>AS-05</td><td>One Swarn process operates one workspace at a time.</td><td>The workspace, dataset registry, model registry and session store are all per-process singletons. Concurrent processes will not see each other's state.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>AS-06</td><td>The deployed model is capable of reliable tool/function calling.</td><td>Review parsing, reflection and the whole loop depend on forced tool calls. A weaker model degrades the entire system.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.7 Dependencies</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Dependency</th><th style="width:14%">Type</th><th style="width:14%">Required?</th><th>Used by</th></tr>
<tr><td>OpenAI-compatible LLM endpoint</td><td>External service</td><td>Mandatory</td><td>Every LLM call.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">openai</span> SDK</td><td>Library</td><td>Mandatory</td><td>Endpoint client.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">pandas</span>, <span class="m">scikit-learn</span></td><td>Library</td><td>Mandatory</td><td>Data pipeline, feature engineering, training, evaluation.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">fastapi</span>, <span class="m">uvicorn</span>, <span class="m">typer</span></td><td>Library</td><td>Mandatory</td><td>Dashboard and CLI.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">docker</span> SDK + daemon</td><td>Runtime</td><td>Optional</td><td>Isolated execution; falls back to subprocess.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">chromadb</span>, <span class="m">sentence-transformers</span></td><td>Library</td><td>Optional</td><td>Repo-RAG and multimodal RAG.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">xgboost</span>, <span class="m">lightgbm</span>, <span class="m">torch</span>, <span class="m">optuna</span></td><td>Library</td><td>Optional</td><td>Individual training candidates and HPO. Each is isolated so one missing library cannot fail the leaderboard.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">mcp</span> SDK</td><td>Library</td><td>Optional</td><td>MCP client and server.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">pdfplumber</span>, <span class="m">pytesseract</span>, <span class="m">whisper</span></td><td>Library</td><td>Optional</td><td>Multimodal indexing.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">peft</span>, <span class="m">transformers</span>, <span class="m">bitsandbytes</span></td><td>Library</td><td>Optional</td><td>Fine-tuning. <span class="m">bitsandbytes</span> is GPU-only.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">opentelemetry</span> SDK</td><td>Library</td><td>Optional</td><td>Tracing. Absent &rarr; every span method becomes a no-op.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">skl2onnx</span>, <span class="m">onnxruntime</span></td><td>Library</td><td>Optional</td><td>ONNX export; falls back to pickle.</td></tr>
</table>

