<h2>4.3 FR-001 &mdash; ReAct Agent Loop</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall accept a natural-language task and complete it by repeatedly: asking the language model what to do next given the conversation so far, executing every tool the model requests, and feeding the results back, until the model calls <span class="m">finish_task</span> or a cap is reached.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer, Software Engineer, MCP Client, Dashboard.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The deployed LLM endpoint is configured and reachable; the workspace directory exists and is writable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>A <span class="m">Session</span> is closed and persisted; a result dictionary <span class="m">{outcome, summary, session_id}</span> is returned; any files the agent wrote remain in the workspace.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Create a session and seed the conversation with the system prompt and the task.<br>
2. For each iteration up to the cap: recompute the tool definitions from the registry (recomputed every iteration so tools registered mid-run are visible), call the model, record any reasoning text as a <span class="m">PLAN</span> step.<br>
3. For each requested tool call: record a <span class="m">TOOL_CALL</span> step, dispatch it, assess the raw result for errors (FR-005), then scan the assessed result for injection patterns (FR-029) &mdash; strictly in that order, then record a <span class="m">TOOL_RESULT</span> step.<br>
4. Append results to the conversation and repeat.<br>
5. On <span class="m">finish_task</span>, set outcome <span class="m">complete</span>, store the supplied summary, close the session and return.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; The model returns no tool call: the loop terminates with outcome <span class="m">no_tool_use</span>.<br>
A2 &mdash; Conversation exceeds the character budget: older tool results are deterministically truncated head+tail, keeping the last 6 messages verbatim, and the loop continues.<br>
A3 &mdash; Repetition is detected: a corrective note is injected and the loop continues (FR-007).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Iteration cap reached: outcome <span class="m">max_iterations</span>, an <span class="m">ERROR</span> step is recorded with reason <span class="m">max_iterations_reached</span>.<br>
E2 &mdash; Three consecutive tool errors with no success in between: outcome recorded with reason <span class="m">max_consecutive_errors_reached</span>, run aborts.<br>
E3 &mdash; LLM call fails all retries: <span class="m">LLMError</span> propagates and the run ends abnormally.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Critical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-002, FR-004, FR-005, FR-006, FR-007, FR-029.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; A task that completes normally returns outcome <span class="m">complete</span> with a non-empty summary and a resolvable session id.<br>
AC2 &mdash; A run in which the model never calls a tool returns <span class="m">no_tool_use</span> and exits non-zero via the CLI.<br>
AC3 &mdash; A tool registered into the registry <i>during</i> a run is visible to the model on the next iteration of that same run.<br>
AC4 &mdash; With the cap set to N, the loop makes at most N model calls.<br>
AC5 &mdash; Self-correction runs before the guardrail scan; a result that is both a genuine error and contains an injection phrase is still classified as an error.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> Iteration cap default 30 (<span class="m">SWARN_MAX_ITERATIONS</span>); context budget default 400,000 characters. AC5 was a real defect, found and fixed during development. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; it has no regression test (&sect;16.8).</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.4 FR-002 &mdash; Tool Registry and Dispatch</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall maintain one central registry mapping a tool name to its description, JSON input schema and callable, shall expose a filtered view of it to the model, and shall dispatch calls by name without ever raising into the caller.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop, orchestrator roles, MCP integration layer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>Tool modules are imported (registration happens at import time via decorator).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>The requested tool has executed and returned a string; or an error string describing the failure has been returned.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. A decorator registers each function with its description and schema at import time.<br>
2. The loop requests tool definitions, optionally filtered by an allow-list.<br>
3. Definitions are emitted in the internal (Anthropic-style) format and translated to OpenAI function-calling format on the wire by the endpoint client.<br>
4. Dispatch looks up the callable and invokes it with the supplied arguments.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; When the allow-list contains <span class="m">connect_mcp_server</span>, all dynamically registered <span class="m">mcp_*</span> tools are additionally included. Roles without that tool never gain this visibility.<br>
A2 &mdash; No allow-list supplied: the full registry is returned.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Unknown tool name: an error string is returned.<br>
E2 &mdash; The tool raises: caught and returned as <span class="m">Error running '&lt;name&gt;': &lt;Type&gt;: &lt;message&gt;</span>.<br>
E3 &mdash; File path outside the workspace: rejected by the path guard.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Critical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>None (foundational).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; 45 tools are registered at import time.<br>
AC2 &mdash; No tool invocation propagates an exception to the loop under any input.<br>
AC3 &mdash; A tool registered at runtime is visible to a role holding <span class="m">connect_mcp_server</span> and absent from a role that does not.<br>
AC4 &mdash; A path containing traversal segments resolving outside the workspace is rejected.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> 45 statically registered tools plus dynamic <span class="m">mcp_&lt;server&gt;_&lt;tool&gt;</span> entries. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no test module exists for the registry; AC3 and AC4 were manually verified only (&sect;16.8).</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.5 FR-003 &mdash; Sandboxed Code Execution</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall execute agent-generated Python and shell commands in an isolated environment, enforce a per-call timeout, cap captured output, and return a structured result carrying exit code, timeout flag and elapsed time.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop (via <span class="m">run_python</span>, <span class="m">run_shell</span>, <span class="m">install_package</span>), search engine.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A workspace directory exists. Docker daemon reachable if the Docker backend is selected.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>An <span class="m">ExecResult</span> is returned; any files the code wrote persist in the workspace; no container is left running after a timeout.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Select a backend: forced by <span class="m">SWARN_SANDBOX</span>, otherwise auto-detected by pinging the Docker daemon, otherwise subprocess.<br>
2. Write the code to a file inside the scoped working directory.<br>
3. Execute with the effective timeout (per-call override, else the default).<br>
4. Capture stdout and stderr, truncating head+tail at the output cap.<br>
5. Return <span class="m">ExecResult(output, exit_code, timed_out, exec_time)</span>.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Docker unavailable at start: silently fall back to subprocess.<br>
A2 &mdash; Output exceeds the cap: middle content replaced with a truncation marker stating how many characters were removed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Subprocess timeout: exit code <span class="m">-1</span>, <span class="m">timed_out=True</span>, partial output preserved.<br>
E2 &mdash; Docker timeout: the container is killed and recycled (Docker cannot kill a single exec), exit code <span class="m">-1</span>, empty output.<br>
E3 &mdash; Non-zero exit: surfaced as <span class="m">[exit N]</span> in the legacy display string. Execution failures are never retried at this layer.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Critical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-002.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Default per-call timeout is 300 s (<span class="m">SWARN_EXEC_TIMEOUT</span>); search nodes default to 600 s.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Output is capped at 50,000 characters with head+tail preservation.<br>
AC3 &mdash; After a Docker timeout, no container from that exec remains running (verified by container listing).<br>
AC4 &mdash; The subprocess backend runs correctly on Windows.<br>
AC5 &mdash; Docker containers are constrained to 2 GB memory and 2 CPUs, with the workspace bind-mounted read-write and nothing else.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> The Docker timeout leak (a timed-out exec left eating CPU) was a real defect, fixed in V3 by container recycling.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.6 FR-004 &mdash; LLM Routing, Retry and Token Accounting</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall route every language-model call to one configured OpenAI-compatible endpoint, retry transient failures with capped exponential backoff, translate between the internal tool format and OpenAI function calling, and accumulate token usage.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>All internal callers: agent loop, orchestrator, search engine, reflection, dashboard, MCP server.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>Endpoint name, base URL and API key are resolved from environment or defaults.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>A normalised response with content blocks is returned; cumulative usage counters are updated.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Resolve the client from cache, keyed by endpoint identity.<br>
2. Issue the call with the requested temperature, token limit, tools and tool-choice.<br>
3. Normalise the response into internal content blocks.<br>
4. Add input, output and cache-read token counts to the running usage total.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Spec is <span class="m">mock</span> or <span class="m">mock:&lt;name&gt;</span>: a scripted offline client is returned instead. This is the only spec that changes routing and is what makes the offline test suite possible.<br>
A2 &mdash; Any other model spec: a one-time notice is printed that the spec is ignored, and the deployed endpoint is used.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Retryable failure (overload, rate limit, 429/500/502/503/529, timeout, connection, "temporarily"): wait and retry, up to 5 attempts.<br>
E2 &mdash; Non-retryable failure: fail immediately.<br>
E3 &mdash; Retries exhausted: raise <span class="m">LLMError("LLM call failed after retries: &lt;cause&gt;")</span>.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Critical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>None.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Maximum 5 attempts per call.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Backoff is <span class="m">min(2^attempt + jitter, 30)</span> seconds, i.e. approximately 1 s, 2 s, 4 s, 8 s with up to 1 s jitter, hard-capped at 30 s.<br>
AC3 &mdash; A non-retryable error fails on the first attempt without sleeping.<br>
AC4 &mdash; Token usage after N calls equals the sum of the per-call reported usage.<br>
AC5 &mdash; Internal tool definitions round-trip correctly to OpenAI function-calling format and back.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no per-request HTTP timeout is configured on the client; the SDK default applies. Because "timeout" is on the retryable list, a hung request could consume all 5 attempts. An explicit timeout should be set.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.7 FR-005 &mdash; Self-Correction on Tool Failure</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall detect whether a tool result represents a failure, classify the failure into one of seven kinds, append targeted corrective guidance to the result before the model sees it, and abort the run after an unbroken streak of failures.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop (one policy instance per session).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A tool has returned a string result.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>An enriched result string is produced; counters and the correction log are updated; a <span class="m">CORRECTION</span> step is recorded.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Detect error, in order: a Python traceback marker; a <span class="m">Error running '&lt;tool&gt;'</span> prefix; an <span class="m">Error: </span> / <span class="m">Error executing</span> prefix; a non-zero <span class="m">[exit N]</span> pattern; a "timed out after" phrase; or any of 24 known Python exception names.<br>
2. Classify, first match wins: SYNTAX &rarr; IMPORT &rarr; FILE &rarr; TIMEOUT &rarr; RUNTIME &rarr; SHELL &rarr; GENERIC.<br>
3. Append a correction block showing the attempt number, the remaining allowance, the error type and one instructive hint.<br>
4. Increment the consecutive counter.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Result is not an error: reset the consecutive counter to zero and return the result unchanged.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Consecutive errors reach the maximum: the policy signals abort and the loop stops rather than continuing to spend model calls.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-001, FR-002.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Maximum consecutive errors is 3.<br>
AC2 &mdash; The policy never retries or auto-fixes anything; it only classifies, annotates and counts. Deciding the fix remains the model's job.<br>
AC3 &mdash; A single isolated error followed by a success does not trigger abort.<br>
AC4 &mdash; Each of the seven error kinds is correctly classified from a representative sample.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented, untested.</b> <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no test module exists for this High-priority requirement. None of AC1&ndash;AC4 has a regression test (&sect;16.8).</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.8 FR-006 &mdash; Session Memory and Structured Traces</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall record every plan, tool call, tool result, correction event and terminal outcome of a run as a typed step, persist the completed run as machine-readable and human-readable files, maintain a bounded index of recent runs, and publish steps live to subscribers as they occur.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop, dashboard, <span class="m">list_sessions</span> / <span class="m">recall_session</span> tools.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The sessions directory is writable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td><span class="m">sessions/&lt;uuid&gt;/trace.json</span> and <span class="m">sessions/&lt;uuid&gt;/summary.md</span> exist; <span class="m">sessions/index.json</span> contains an upserted entry.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Create a session with a UUID and attach all globally registered step subscribers.<br>
2. On every step, append it and fire each subscriber callback synchronously.<br>
3. On close, write the trace and summary files and upsert the index.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; A subscriber raises: the exception is swallowed. A broken dashboard subscriber must never break an agent run.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; The run is killed before close: no trace or summary file is written for that run. Live subscribers still saw every step. This is a known and accepted characteristic.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-001.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Six step kinds are supported: plan, tool_call, tool_result, correction, complete, error.<br>
AC2 &mdash; The index retains the 100 most recent runs, most recent first, with no duplicate ids.<br>
AC3 &mdash; The live-callback list is excluded from persistence and does not break JSON serialisation.<br>
AC4 &mdash; A subscriber receives steps of a still-running session before any file exists on disk.<br>
AC5 &mdash; A deliberately raising subscriber does not crash the run.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented, untested.</b> <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; (a) no test module exists for session memory; AC2&ndash;AC5 were manually verified only (&sect;16.8). (b) Defect: the persisted step key is <span class="m">time</span> while the live WebSocket payload uses <span class="m">timestamp</span>, and the dashboard's detail view reads <span class="m">timestamp</span> from the persisted trace, producing an invalid date. Unify the key.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.9 FR-007 &mdash; Doom-Loop Detection and Context Compaction</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall detect when the agent repeats the same tool call and receives the same result, inject a corrective note, and independently compact old tool results once the conversation exceeds a character budget.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>At least two comparable tool calls have occurred.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>Either a corrective note has been injected, or the conversation has been truncated, or neither.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Hash each tool call together with its result.<br>
2. On a repeat of the same call producing the same result, inject a corrective note telling the agent to change approach.<br>
3. Independently, when total conversation characters exceed the budget, truncate older tool results head+tail while keeping the most recent messages verbatim.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Same call, <i>different</i> result (for example a polling operation): not treated as repetition. Result-hash awareness is what prevents this false positive.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>None. Both mechanisms are advisory; neither terminates a run.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-001, FR-006.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Character budget default is 400,000 (<span class="m">SWARN_CONTEXT_CHAR_BUDGET</span>).<br>
AC2 &mdash; The last 6 messages are never truncated; older results keep 700 leading and 500 trailing characters.<br>
AC3 &mdash; A polling pattern that returns changing results does not trigger the repetition guard.<br>
AC4 &mdash; Truncation is deterministic and produces identical output for identical input.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> Note that this requirement spans two modules: repetition detection lives in <span class="m">doom_loop.py</span> and is covered by the test suite; context compaction (AC1, AC2, AC4) is implemented in <span class="m">agent_loop.py</span> and is <span class="todo">not covered</span>.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.10 FR-008 &mdash; Repository Semantic Indexing and Search</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall index a directory tree into semantically-meaningful chunks, embed them with a local model, store them in an embedded vector database, and return the most semantically similar chunks for a natural-language query.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop (via <span class="m">index_project</span>, <span class="m">search_codebase</span>), operator (via <span class="m">swarn index</span>).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The vector-store and embedding libraries are installed; the embedding model is available locally or downloadable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>The persistent vector store contains chunks with file, line-range and type metadata.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Walk the tree, skipping vendor and VCS directories.<br>
2. Split files into roughly function- or class-sized chunks rather than fixed-length slices, so a chunk is never a context-free fragment of a function.<br>
3. Embed each chunk locally with <span class="m">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span>.<br>
4. Persist to the embedded store.<br>
5. On query, embed the query identically and return nearest neighbours with citations.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Multimodal chunks (FR-027) are present: they are returned blended with code chunks, ranked purely by relevance, through the same unmodified search call.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Libraries missing: a clean dependency error string.<br>
E2 &mdash; Model download fails in a restricted or offline environment: a clean, actionable error string naming the model and the likely cause.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-002.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; A query semantically related to code that shares no literal keywords with it still returns that code.<br>
AC2 &mdash; No failure path raises; every failure returns a string, honouring DC-02.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Results carry file path and line range.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Partial.</b> Core code indexing and search work. The offline-failure path was a real defect (an unhandled OS error from the model download escaped through every indexing tool) and has been fixed and verified. First-run behaviour still depends on outbound network access to a model host.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.11 FR-009 &mdash; Solution-Tree Search Engine</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall solve an ML task by systematic experimentation: generating several independent complete solution scripts, executing each, scoring each, debugging the broken ones and making atomic improvements to the best working one, until a budget is exhausted.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer (via <span class="m">swarn solve</span>), agent loop (via the <span class="m">solve_ml_task</span> tool), MCP Client.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A task description is supplied; a data directory is supplied unless resuming; the endpoint is reachable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>A run directory exists containing the journal, the best solution script and a report; a <span class="m">SearchResult</span> is returned.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Prepare the run directory and copy (or symlink) the data directory into the workspace as <span class="m">input/</span>.<br>
2. Build the data preview and, if enabled, load the playbook and the most similar past runs as prior art.<br>
3. Select the next action by policy: if fewer than <span class="m">num_drafts</span> roots exist &rarr; <b>draft</b>; else if a buggy leaf exists &rarr; <b>debug</b> with probability <span class="m">debug_prob</span>, bounded by <span class="m">max_debug_depth</span> consecutive fixes per branch; else &rarr; <b>improve</b> one top-k good node, epsilon-greedy.<br>
4. Generate code; apply the static gate (FR-012); execute in the sandbox; review the result (FR-011).<br>
5. Append the node and save the journal.<br>
6. Repeat until the step, time or token budget is reached.<br>
7. Select the best node, write the best solution and the report, and optionally reflect (FR-018).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Parallel workers configured: actions are pipelined across a thread pool with reservation-aware policy (FR-013).<br>
A2 &mdash; Resuming an existing run: the journal is loaded and additional steps are appended (FR-014).<br>
A3 &mdash; Learning disabled: no playbook or prior-art injection and no reflection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; No good node exists at the end: no best-solution file is written; the report records the failure and recent failures are listed.<br>
E2 &mdash; Node execution times out: the node is scored buggy and the branch may be debugged.<br>
E3 &mdash; Token budget exceeded mid-run: the search stops cleanly and reports what it produced.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Critical &mdash; this is the headline capability of the product.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-003, FR-004, FR-010, FR-011, FR-012, FR-015, FR-016, FR-018.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Defaults: 20 steps, 4 drafts, debug probability 0.5, max debug depth 3, improve top-k 2, node execution timeout 600 s.<br>
AC2 &mdash; The policy transitions draft &rarr; debug/improve exactly as specified, verified by state-transition tests.<br>
AC3 &mdash; A full end-to-end search completes offline against synthetic data using a mock model and real execution.<br>
AC4 &mdash; The journal is saved after every node, so killing the process at any point leaves a resumable run.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> Covered by end-to-end offline tests, sequential and parallel, including resume and token budgets.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.12 FR-010 &mdash; Solution Journal and Persistence</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall record every solution attempt as a node in a tree carrying its plan, code, execution output, timing, exit status, review analysis, metric, metric direction and buggy/good verdict, and shall persist the whole tree after every step.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Search engine, report generator, dashboard.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A run directory exists.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td><span class="m">runs/&lt;run_id&gt;/journal.json</span> reflects the complete tree.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Create a node with a short unique id and a parent reference.<br>
2. Append it; the step number is assigned from the current tree size.<br>
3. Write the journal to disk.<br>
4. On request, resolve the best node among good nodes.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Best-node selection with mixed metric directions: the direction is decided by majority vote across good nodes, then minimum or maximum is taken accordingly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; A corrupt journal file: the dashboard listing swallows the error and reports zero nodes rather than failing the whole listing.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Critical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-009.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; A node is <i>good</i> only when it is not buggy <b>and</b> has a non-null metric. Nodes default to buggy until reviewed.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Node serialisation round-trips without field loss or renaming.<br>
AC3 &mdash; The journal file is written after every single node, not at the end of the run.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> <span class="assume">[Assumption: the majority-vote tie-break on metric direction is intended behaviour. It is unusual &mdash; a task-level declaration of direction would be more predictable. Confirm before treating it as a requirement rather than an implementation detail.]</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.13 FR-011 &mdash; Forced-Review Scoring of a Solution</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall score each executed solution by making a second, low-temperature model call that is <i>forced</i> via tool choice to return a structured verdict &mdash; bug flag, summary, metric and metric direction &mdash; and shall prefer the script's own printed metric line over the reviewer's number when the two disagree.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Search engine.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A node's code has been executed and terminal output captured.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>The node carries a verdict, analysis, metric and direction.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Truncate terminal output to the review character limit.<br>
2. Call the feedback model at low temperature with forced tool choice.<br>
3. Parse the structured verdict.<br>
4. Regex-extract the script's own <span class="m">Final Validation Metric:</span> line; where it disagrees with the reviewer, the printed line wins.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; No metric line printed: the reviewer's metric is used if the reviewer did not flag a bug.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Reviewer returns no parseable verdict: the node remains buggy (the pessimistic default).<br>
E2 &mdash; Review model call fails all retries: the node remains buggy.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Critical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-004, FR-009, FR-010.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Review temperature is 0.2; code-generation temperature is 0.7.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Terminal output shown to the reviewer is capped at 6,000 characters.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Where a printed metric and a reviewer metric conflict, the printed value is recorded.<br>
AC4 &mdash; An unparseable review never promotes a node to good.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> Review parsing is unit-tested.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.14 FR-012 &mdash; Static Pre-Execution Gate</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall analyse generated code before executing it and score it buggy without consuming a sandbox execution when it contains a syntax error, omits the required metric print, or contains a guaranteed hang.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Search engine.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>Code has been generated; the gate is enabled.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>Either the code proceeds to execution, or a buggy node is recorded with the static reason.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Parse the code to an AST.<br>
2. Reject on parse failure.<br>
3. Reject when no final-metric print is present.<br>
4. Reject on guaranteed-hang patterns.<br>
5. Otherwise pass through to execution.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Gate disabled by configuration: every candidate is executed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>None &mdash; the gate is a pure function over source text.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-009.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Enabled by default.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Syntactically invalid code produces a buggy node with zero sandbox executions.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Valid code is never rejected by the gate (no false positives in the test corpus).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> Unit-tested.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.15 FR-013 &mdash; Parallel Search Execution</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall pipeline the propose &rarr; execute &rarr; review cycle across a thread pool, using reservation-aware policy so that two workers never debug the same node and drafting does not run away.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer (via <span class="m">--workers</span>), search engine.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>Worker count greater than one.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>The journal contains the same node semantics as a sequential run, produced in less wall-clock time.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. The scheduler selects the next action under the same policy as sequential search.<br>
2. Before dispatch, the target node is reserved so no other worker selects it.<br>
3. Workers execute concurrently; completed nodes are appended and the journal saved.<br>
4. Reservations are released on completion.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; All candidate targets are reserved: the scheduler falls back to drafting, bounded by the draft limit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; A worker raises: the node is recorded as buggy and the pool continues.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High &mdash; this is a stated competitive differentiator.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-009, FR-010.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Default worker count 1, configurable by flag or <span class="m">SWARN_SEARCH_WORKERS</span>.<br>
AC2 &mdash; No two concurrent workers debug the same node (verified by reservation tests).<br>
AC3 &mdash; The number of root drafts never exceeds <span class="m">num_drafts</span> regardless of worker count.<br>
AC4 &mdash; Four drafts at four workers complete in approximately the wall time of one draft.<br>
AC5 &mdash; The journal remains internally consistent under concurrency.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> Parallel reservation transitions and a full parallel end-to-end search are both covered by tests. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; AC4 is a design claim from the HLD; no measured speed-up figure exists. Benchmark it before quoting it externally.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.16 FR-014 &mdash; Checkpoint and Resume</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall allow a killed or interrupted search run to be continued from its persisted journal, adding a specified number of further nodes to the existing tree.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer (via <span class="m">--resume &lt;run_id&gt;</span>).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A run directory with a readable journal exists.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>The same run directory contains the extended tree, a refreshed best solution and a regenerated report.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Locate the run directory by id.<br>
2. Load the journal and rebuild the node index.<br>
3. Continue the normal policy loop for the requested additional steps.<br>
4. Rewrite the best solution and report.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Resume is supplied, so the data-directory argument is not required.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Run id not found: the command reports the error and exits non-zero.<br>
E2 &mdash; Journal unreadable or corrupt: <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; recovery behaviour for a partially-written journal is not specified in the design material. Define it.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-009, FR-010.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; <span class="m">--steps</span> on a resume means <i>additional</i> nodes, not a total.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Resumed nodes correctly reference parents from the original run.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Resume is covered by an offline end-to-end test.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.17 FR-015 &mdash; Budget Enforcement</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall enforce explicit, user-visible budgets on a run &mdash; number of steps, wall-clock time and total tokens &mdash; and shall stop cleanly and report partial results rather than failing when a budget is reached.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer, Platform Operator.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>None; step budget always applies.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>The run terminates with a complete journal and report describing what was achieved within budget.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Before each node, check remaining steps, remaining wall-clock time and accumulated tokens.<br>
2. When a time budget is set, shrink the per-node execution timeout so the node can fit in the remaining time.<br>
3. When any budget is exhausted, stop scheduling new nodes and finalise.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; No time or token budget set: only the step budget applies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Budget exhausted before any good node exists: the run finalises with no best solution and a report explaining this.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High &mdash; stated as a transparency differentiator against opaque credit systems.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-004 (token accounting), FR-009.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; A token budget stops a run before it is exceeded, verified end-to-end offline.<br>
AC2 &mdash; A time budget shrinks per-node timeouts rather than allowing a single node to overrun the whole budget.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Budget exhaustion is a clean stop, never an exception.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.18 FR-016 &mdash; Data Preview Injection</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall inject a preview of the actual data &mdash; file tree, column dtypes and sample rows &mdash; into every code-generation prompt, so generated code targets real column names rather than guessed ones.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Search engine.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A data directory has been prepared in the run workspace.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>Every generation prompt contains the preview.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Walk the input directory and render the file tree.<br>
2. For tabular files, read dtypes and the first rows.<br>
3. Render as compact text and include it in the prompt.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Non-tabular data: only the file tree is included.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; A file cannot be parsed: it is listed in the tree without a preview; the run continues.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-009.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Generated code references column names that exist in the supplied data.<br>
AC2 &mdash; A directory containing no readable tabular file still produces a valid prompt.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.19 FR-017 &mdash; Run Report Generation</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall produce a human-readable markdown report for every search run containing the task, outcome, search statistics, an ASCII rendering of the solution tree, a metric-history table, the best solution's plan and analysis, and the most recent failures.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer, Manager, dashboard.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A run has finished or been resumed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td><span class="m">runs/&lt;run_id&gt;/report.md</span> exists.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>Render, in order: title, task, outcome, search statistics, solution tree (fenced ASCII), metric history (step / stage / metric / execution time), best plan, best analysis, recent failures.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; No good node: the metric history and best-solution sections are omitted or empty, and the failure section carries the explanation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>None.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-010.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; The report lists at most the 5 most recent buggy nodes.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Every good node appears exactly once in the metric-history table.<br>
AC3 &mdash; The report is regenerated on resume.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.20 FR-018 &mdash; Cross-Run Knowledge and Playbook</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall, after a finished run, distil generalisable lessons via a low-cost model call, maintain them in a hard-capped playbook, archive the run in a full-text-searchable index, and inject the playbook plus the most similar past runs into future runs as prior art.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Search engine, ML Engineer (via <span class="m">swarn playbook</span>), dashboard.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>Reflection is enabled; the knowledge directory is writable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>The playbook file and the run archive are updated.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. On run completion, call the feedback model at low temperature with a forced <span class="m">submit_lessons</span> tool call.<br>
2. Keep at most 5 lessons from the call.<br>
3. Normalise whitespace, truncate each lesson, case-insensitively de-duplicate.<br>
4. Evict oldest-first until the rendered playbook fits its character cap.<br>
5. Insert the run into the full-text index.<br>
6. On a future run, prepend the playbook and up to 3 similar past runs to the prompts.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Learning disabled (<span class="m">--no-learn</span>): neither injection nor reflection occurs.<br>
A2 &mdash; <span class="m">swarn playbook --clear</span>: the playbook file is removed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Any database error: swallowed; an empty result is returned. Knowledge is explicitly best-effort and must never fail a run.<br>
E2 &mdash; Reflection call fails: the run still completes successfully.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High &mdash; stated as the key differentiator ("HeyNeo starts every task cold; Swarn gets smarter with use").</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-004, FR-009.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Playbook is capped at 6,000 characters; each lesson at 300 characters; at most 5 lessons are accepted per run; at most 3 similar runs are injected.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Eviction is oldest-first and the cap is never exceeded.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Duplicate lessons differing only in case or whitespace are not stored twice.<br>
AC4 &mdash; A database failure returns empty and does not raise.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; defect: the archive table has no primary key or unique constraint, so re-indexing the same run id silently appends duplicate rows, which will skew similarity retrieval over time. Add a uniqueness constraint or an upsert.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.21 FR-019 &mdash; Data Ingestion and Validation</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall load tabular data from CSV, Excel, Parquet, SQL and cloud object storage into a named in-process registry, and shall produce a data-quality report covering dtypes, nulls, duplicate rows, numeric outliers and optional schema validation.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop (9 tools), ML Engineer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The source is reachable and the relevant optional driver is installed for cloud or SQL sources.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>A named dataset exists in the registry; a validation report string has been returned.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. A connector reads the source into a dataframe.<br>
2. It is registered under the caller's chosen name; the response states the shape and columns and prompts validation as the next step.<br>
3. Validation reports dtypes; per-column null count and percentage; exact full-row duplicates; numeric outliers by z-score beyond 3 standard deviations; and a schema check if the schema library is installed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Schema library absent: validation degrades gracefully and reports the rest.<br>
A2 &mdash; <span class="m">save_dataset</span> writes a registered dataset back to CSV or Parquet.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Source unreadable: an error string is returned.<br>
E2 &mdash; Cloud driver not installed: an actionable error naming the package to install.<br>
E3 &mdash; Validation never raises and never blocks &mdash; it always returns a report, including when everything is clean.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-002.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; All five connectors register through one common path and produce a consistent description.<br>
AC2 &mdash; A clean dataset yields an explicit "no nulls / no outliers detected" report rather than silence.<br>
AC3 &mdash; The outlier rule is z-score with a threshold of 3.0, applied to numeric columns only.<br>
AC4 &mdash; Validation is advisory: it never prevents downstream tools from running.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.22 FR-020 &mdash; Feature Profiling and Engineering</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall profile every column and <i>suggest</i> a role, and shall separately <i>apply</i> a deterministic, fitted transformation pipeline only when explicitly instructed which columns to drop.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop, ML Engineer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A dataset is registered; a target column is named.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>Either a text profile has been returned, or a model-ready numeric matrix has been produced and the fitted transformer retained.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow (profile)</td><td>Classify each column: single distinct value &rarr; constant, drop; all values distinct and integer or string &rarr; likely identifier, drop; datetime-shaped &rarr; decompose; numeric &rarr; impute and scale; low-cardinality categorical &rarr; one-hot; high-cardinality categorical &rarr; frequency-encode. Return as suggestions only &mdash; nothing is dropped or transformed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Main Flow (engineer)</td><td>Build a column transformer: numeric &rarr; median-impute then standard-scale; low-cardinality categorical &rarr; most-frequent-impute then one-hot; high-cardinality categorical &rarr; frequency-encode manually to avoid a dummy-column explosion; datetime &rarr; decompose into year, month, day, day-of-week and is-weekend, then route through the numeric branch. Retain the fitted transformer on the engine instance.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; The caller overrides the suggested drops with its own judgement. This separation is deliberate: diagnosis is deterministic code, the decision remains the agent's.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Target column absent: error string.<br>
E2 &mdash; String-dtype detection must use the string-dtype predicate, not the object-dtype predicate. Under pandas 3.0 the latter silently stops matching string columns, causing date-like and identifier columns to be misclassified.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-019.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Profiling never mutates the dataset.<br>
AC2 &mdash; The fitted transformer is retained so the identical transform can be applied to new data without re-fitting. Re-fitting on a test set leaks information and must be structurally prevented.<br>
AC3 &mdash; High-cardinality categoricals do not produce one-hot columns.<br>
AC4 &mdash; String columns are correctly detected under pandas 3.0.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> The pandas 3.0 dtype issue was a real defect found and fixed.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.23 FR-021 &mdash; Model Training and Hyperparameter Optimisation</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall detect the task type from the target column, split the data, train every requested candidate family in isolation, rank them by the metric appropriate to the task, register the winner as a trained artifact, and optionally run a hyperparameter study over one family.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop, ML Engineer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A dataset is registered and a target column named.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>A leaderboard has been returned; the winning artifact is registered together with its metrics, feature columns, target column and held-out split.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Detect task type: numeric target with more than 20 distinct values &rarr; regression; exactly 2 distinct &rarr; binary classification; 3&ndash;20 distinct &rarr; multiclass classification; everything else &rarr; regression, as an undocumented fallback.<br>
2. Split 80/20, stratified for classification.<br>
3. Fit each candidate (linear/logistic, random forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, and a small neural network) inside its own try/except so one missing or broken library cannot fail the leaderboard &mdash; the failed row is reported with its error and the rest are ranked normally.<br>
4. Rank by RMSE (lower better) for regression or accuracy (higher better) for classification.<br>
5. Store the winner with its held-out split attached.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Hyperparameter tuning: run an Optuna study over a small fixed search space per family, reusing the identical split logic, and register the result under a <span class="m">&lt;name&gt;__&lt;candidate&gt;_tuned</span> identifier.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Candidate library missing: that row records "not installed"; other candidates continue.<br>
E2 &mdash; Candidate raises: that row records the exception type and message; other candidates continue.<br>
E3 &mdash; Every candidate fails: a leaderboard of failures is returned with no winner.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-019, FR-020.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Per-candidate isolation is verified: with one candidate's library deliberately broken, the other candidates still train and rank.<br>
AC2 &mdash; The held-out split is stored on the artifact so evaluation scores the exact rows the reported metrics came from. Re-splitting at evaluation time would silently produce a different test set.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Task detection matches the stated thresholds exactly.<br>
AC4 &mdash; Tuning reuses the same split logic as training.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> AC1 was demonstrated by a real broken PyTorch install during development, not by a synthetic test alone. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the fallback branch is a latent correctness issue: a non-numeric target with more than 20 distinct values (a free-text or high-cardinality string column) is silently classified as <i>regression</i> rather than rejected. This will produce a nonsensical model instead of an error. Make the fallback explicit and reject targets it cannot classify.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.24 FR-022 &mdash; Model Evaluation and Visualisation</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall produce a diagnostic report for a trained artifact and shall render confusion matrix, ROC curve, residual and cross-model comparison plots as image files with explanatory captions.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop, Reviewer role, ML Engineer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A trained artifact exists.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>PNG files exist under <span class="m">workspace/plots/</span>; a caption string is returned.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Resolve the artifact through the trainer, so evaluation can never be stale relative to what has been trained.<br>
2. Predict on the stored held-out split.<br>
3. Compute metrics; build the figure on a headless rendering backend; save the PNG; return a caption explaining how to read it.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Cross-model comparison groups artifacts by task type before charting, because a regression error and a classification accuracy are not on the same scale and must not share one axis.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Unknown artifact: error string.<br>
E2 &mdash; Prediction must inspect the model's module path before importing a deep-learning framework. Importing unconditionally crashed every evaluation call in an environment where that framework was installed but its native libraries were broken.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-021.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Evaluation on a non-deep-learning model succeeds even when the deep-learning framework is installed but broken.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Plot rendering succeeds with no display attached.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Metrics reported by evaluation equal the metrics reported by training for the same artifact.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> AC1 corresponds to a real defect found and fixed.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.25 FR-023 &mdash; Deployment Packaging</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall package a trained artifact into a self-contained folder containing the serialised model, a generated typed prediction service, a dependency file, a container definition and metadata.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop, ML Engineer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>A trained artifact exists.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td><span class="m">workspace/deployments/&lt;artifact_id&gt;/</span> contains the model file, <span class="m">app.py</span>, <span class="m">requirements.txt</span>, <span class="m">Dockerfile</span> and <span class="m">metadata.json</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Serialise the model, by default via joblib.<br>
2. Generate a service exposing a typed prediction endpoint whose input schema fields are derived directly from the artifact's feature columns, so the API contract cannot drift from what the model expects.<br>
3. Generate the dependency file and container definition <i>for the deployed service</i>, deliberately separate from Swarn's own dependencies.<br>
4. Write metadata capturing artifact id, candidate, task type, metrics, feature columns, target column, model format and filename.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; ONNX export requested: attempted only for eligible native estimator families; otherwise the function explains why and falls back to pickle rather than guessing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Unknown artifact: error string.<br>
E2 &mdash; Generated code must be compiled before being trusted (DC-09). A template indentation defect produced invalid Python that was invisible on inspection and was caught only by compiling the generated file.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-021.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; The generated service compiles.<br>
AC2 &mdash; The generated input schema fields exactly match the artifact's feature columns.<br>
AC3 &mdash; ONNX export on an ineligible model family falls back to pickle with an explanation, not an error.<br>
AC4 &mdash; The package runs without Swarn installed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.26 FR-024 &mdash; Multi-Agent Orchestration</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall execute a task through four specialised roles &mdash; Planner, Coder, Reviewer and optionally Tester &mdash; each running the same agent loop with its own system prompt and its own restricted slice of the tool registry, routing rejections back to the Coder and terminating after a revision limit.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer (via <span class="m">swarn team</span> or the REPL <span class="m">team</span> command).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The endpoint is reachable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>A final outcome, the blackboard state and a markdown report are returned.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Planner produces a numbered plan. It holds read-only and memory tools; it cannot write files or run code.<br>
2. Coder receives the plan as a written summary and executes it with the full toolset.<br>
3. Reviewer receives the Coder's summary and returns a verdict. It holds read-only and evaluation tools; it cannot edit anything.<br>
4. On approval, Tester runs (if enabled) with sandbox, read and evaluate tools.<br>
5. On pass, the run completes.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Reviewer rejects: route back to the <b>Coder</b>, not the Planner, folding the specific feedback into the next Coder task. Re-planning from scratch over an implementation issue would discard a good plan.<br>
A2 &mdash; Tester fails: route back to the Coder.<br>
A3 &mdash; Tester disabled: approval completes the run directly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Revision limit reached: the orchestrator stops and reports.<br>
E2 &mdash; Ambiguous verdict containing neither an approval nor a rejection keyword: <b>treated as a rejection</b>. Failing safe is preferred to failing convenient.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-001, FR-002.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Roles do not share a message history. Each gets a fresh loop, session and correction policy, and sees only a written summary of the previous role's work.<br>
AC2 &mdash; The Reviewer's tool list contains no file-writing tool. Without this, "review" is just a second coding pass and provides no independent check.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Role prompts are constructed at import time from one shared core in the single system-prompt module, so they cannot drift out of sync.<br>
AC4 &mdash; Rejection keywords are evaluated before approval keywords, and an ambiguous verdict resolves to rejection.<br>
AC5 &mdash; All routing transitions are verified without live model calls.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Partial.</b> The routing logic is fully implemented and was manually verified during development using a scripted stand-in for the agent loop. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; two gaps: (a) that verification has no committed test, so AC4's fail-safe behaviour is unprotected against regression (&sect;16.8); (b) the <i>quality</i> of the four role prompts has never been evaluated against the live endpoint. Routing correctness and role effectiveness are different claims, and neither is currently evidenced by an automated check.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.27 FR-025 &mdash; External MCP Server Integration (Client)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall connect to any external MCP server, discover its tools, register them into the same tool registry under a namespaced prefix, dispatch calls to them synchronously, and disconnect cleanly.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop (Coder role and unrestricted single-agent mode).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The MCP SDK is installed; the server command is executable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>Remote tools appear in the registry as <span class="m">mcp_&lt;server&gt;_&lt;tool&gt;</span> and are callable exactly like local tools.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Start one persistent background event loop for the process lifetime.<br>
2. Start <b>one dedicated long-running task per connected server</b> which owns that connection for its entire lifetime and services requests from a queue.<br>
3. On connect, list the remote tools and register one registry entry per tool, taking the description and JSON schema directly from the server.<br>
4. Synchronous callers place a request on the server's queue and block on a future.<br>
5. On disconnect, place a sentinel on the queue and wait for the owning task to close the connection itself.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Reconnecting under the same name after a disconnect succeeds and leaves the system in a clean, reusable state.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; A connection opened in one task must never be closed from another. Doing so raises a cancel-scope error from the underlying async library, because a cancel scope is bound to the specific task that entered it. This constraint dictates the one-task-per-server design.<br>
E2 &mdash; Server fails to start: an error string is returned and nothing is registered.<br>
E3 &mdash; Remote call timeout: the bridge times out and returns an error string.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-002.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; A real external server can be connected as a subprocess, its tools called through the normal dispatch path, disconnected, and reconnected under the same name.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Newly registered remote tools are visible mid-run to a role holding <span class="m">connect_mcp_server</span> and remain invisible to a role that does not.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Remote tools honour the "errors are strings" contract.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Partial.</b> Fully working over stdio and manually verified against a real server during development, though with no committed test (&sect;16.8). <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; two documented limitations remain: (a) stdio is the only transport; SSE and HTTP are not wired up; (b) server configurations are not persisted, so every restart requires reconnecting manually.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.28 FR-026 &mdash; Swarn as an MCP Server</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall expose its task-execution capability to any MCP client over stdio via four tools: submit a task, poll its status, retrieve its full message transcript, and list all tasks.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>MCP Client (Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf, Zed).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The MCP SDK is installed; the client has registered the server command.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>A background task has been started and can be polled; run artifacts are written by the underlying engine as normal.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. The client submits a task, optionally with a data directory, a step budget and a mode.<br>
2. Mode <span class="m">auto</span> resolves to tree search when a data directory is supplied, otherwise to the single-agent loop.<br>
3. A task record is created with a short id and executed on a daemon thread.<br>
4. Progress messages accumulate on the record; the client polls status or retrieves messages.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Search mode reports per-node progress lines carrying the node number, stage and either a buggy marker or the metric.<br>
A2 &mdash; Agent mode captures standard output and retains the most recent lines as the transcript.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Unknown task id: a plain "Unknown task id" string.<br>
E2 &mdash; The task raises: status becomes <span class="m">failed</span> and the result carries the exception type and message.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-001, FR-009.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Exactly four tools are exposed, all returning plain strings.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Status values are limited to <span class="m">running</span>, <span class="m">complete</span> and <span class="m">failed</span>.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Transcript retrieval returns at most the 100 most recent messages; agent-mode capture retains at most 200 lines.<br>
AC4 &mdash; Submission returns immediately with a task id; it does not block until completion.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Partial.</b> Functionally complete over stdio. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; three gaps: (a) task state is held in memory only and is lost on restart, with no pruning, so it grows unbounded within a session; (b) the tool documentation advertises provider-prefixed model specs that the router ignores (DC-03) &mdash; the documentation is misleading and should be corrected; (c) there is no authentication, which is acceptable for stdio but must be revisited if a network transport is ever added.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.29 FR-027 &mdash; Multimodal Indexing</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall extend the existing semantic index to PDFs, images and audio, reusing the same vector store, embedder and search entry point rather than building a second search system.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The relevant optional extraction libraries are installed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>New chunks exist in the shared index, tagged with a modality type, and are returned by the unmodified search call.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>PDF &mdash; extract prose and chunk it as text; extract each detected table as a single pipe-delimited chunk preserving row and column alignment. Image &mdash; two independent paths, OCR text and a visual embedding, neither required for the other. Audio &mdash; transcribe and group timestamped segments into roughly 60-second windows rather than re-chunking by line count, since segment boundaries are natural pauses.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; Citation metadata reuses the existing line-range fields, repurposed as a page number for PDFs and a sortable timestamp proxy for audio, so the existing search display needs no change.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Extraction library missing: clean error string.<br>
E2 &mdash; A PDF table drawn without ruled lines is not detected as a table. Visual table detection relies on ruling or consistent column gaps. The data is still indexed via the prose path, just not as one coherent table chunk.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Low</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-008.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; One unmodified search call returns code, PDF, image and audio chunks blended and ranked purely by relevance.<br>
AC2 &mdash; No new search system, collection or embedder is introduced.<br>
AC3 &mdash; Every failure path returns a string.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Partial.</b> PDF indexing was verified end-to-end. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; image visual-content search and audio transcription were <b>not</b> run end-to-end (network restrictions blocked the vision model download; no real audio file was tested). The code follows the same lazy-import and clean-error pattern verified elsewhere, but these two paths must be tested in the target environment before they are relied upon.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.30 FR-028 &mdash; LoRA/QLoRA Fine-Tuning</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall validate a prompt/completion dataset, fine-tune a small local open-weight model using low-rank adaptation, and export either a merged standalone model or the adapter alone.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer, agent loop.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>Training libraries installed; a CUDA GPU present if quantised training is requested.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>Training and validation files exist under the fine-tune run directory; a run status is recorded.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Validate every example <b>before</b> any training begins, so a malformed row is not discovered after twenty minutes of compute.<br>
2. Run pre-flight checks that fail fast before any model download or GPU work.<br>
3. Train with the adapter attached to a frozen base model.<br>
4. Export: merged (a full standalone model directory, larger on disk but loadable by anything expecting a normal model) or adapter-only (small, but the loader must know to apply it).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>None. Notably, quantised training does <b>not</b> silently fall back to unquantised when no GPU is present &mdash; the memory/compute trade-off is the caller's decision to make explicitly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Example missing a required field: rejected with the row index and the keys actually found.<br>
E2 &mdash; Quantised training requested without a CUDA GPU: refused with an explanation and the two available options.<br>
E3 &mdash; Base model download fails: caught and returned as a clean error string; run status recorded as failed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Low</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-002.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Every validation rule and pre-flight check is verified.<br>
AC2 &mdash; A download failure yields a clean error string, never a raw traceback, and the run status is <span class="m">failed</span>.<br>
AC3 &mdash; No silent downgrade from quantised to unquantised training.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Partial.</b> Validation and pre-flight paths are fully verified. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the actual training and merge steps have never been run end-to-end (no GPU and no network access to a base model in the development environment). Whether training converges is unevidenced and needs a real GPU-backed run.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.31 FR-029 &mdash; Prompt-Injection Guardrails</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall scan every tool result for known prompt-injection patterns and, when one is found, prepend a clear warning banner while leaving the original content intact.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>Agent loop, ML Engineer (via the guardrail benchmark and findings tools).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The guardrail policy is active. It is on by default because its cost is near zero.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>A possibly-annotated result is returned; findings are recorded for the session.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. Match the result against the pattern list &mdash; instruction-override attempts, role or mode override attempts, and prompt-extraction attempts.<br>
2. On a match, prepend a banner stating which patterns matched, that the content came from tool output rather than from the operator, and that embedded instructions must be treated as untrusted.<br>
3. Record the finding.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; No match: the result is returned unchanged.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; The scan must run <b>after</b> self-correction assessment, never before. Prepending the banner first stops the string starting with an error prefix, which silently defeats error detection for a call that genuinely failed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-001, FR-005.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Flagged content is annotated, never stripped or blocked. A real injection attempt is exactly what the model needs to see in order to recognise and refuse it.<br>
AC2 &mdash; A result that is both a genuine error and contains an injection phrase is still detected as an error, and carries both the guardrail banner and the correction hint.<br>
AC3 &mdash; The benchmark tests both directions: real patterns must flag, and benign look-alikes (for example a function named <span class="m">ignore_warnings</span>) must not. A guardrail that flags everything is as useless as one that catches nothing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Partial.</b> Implemented and on by default; the ordering defect is fixed but has no regression test (&sect;16.8). <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the pattern list is explicitly small and illustrative, not a research-grade classifier. It offers no protection against novel phrasings and must not be presented to anyone as a security guarantee. If Swarn is ever exposed beyond a trusted operator, this needs replacing with a real classifier.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.32 FR-030 &mdash; Distributed Tracing</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall optionally emit OpenTelemetry spans around every language-model call and every tool call, carrying tool name, duration and success status.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>DevOps / SRE, Platform Operator.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>Tracing enabled by environment variable; the OTel SDK installed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>Spans are exported to the configured collector, or to the console when none is configured.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>Open a span, set the tool name, execute, and on exit record duration in milliseconds and the success flag.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; SDK absent: every span method becomes a clean no-op. Tracing is genuinely optional infrastructure, never a hard dependency.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>None &mdash; tracing must never affect run outcome.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Low</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-001.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Off by default; enabled only when the environment variable is set to <span class="m">1</span>.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Recorded duration matches real elapsed time.<br>
AC3 &mdash; A deliberately failed tool call records success as false.<br>
AC4 &mdash; Tracing is deliberately <i>not</i> built on the session-trace mechanism (FR-006). The two serve different audiences &mdash; human replay versus machine observability tooling &mdash; and forcing one format to serve both would compromise both.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> Verified against the real SDK, not a mock. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the two tracing environment variables are absent from the example environment file and are therefore undiscoverable to a new operator.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.33 FR-031 &mdash; Command Line Interface</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall provide a command-line interface for one-off, script-friendly invocation of every major capability, returning meaningful process exit codes.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer, CI system, Platform Operator.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The package is installed; the console entry point is registered by editable or normal install. Without installing, the module form works identically.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>The requested command has run and the process has exited with a meaningful code.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>Parse the command and options, import the heavy dependencies lazily inside the command body so that <span class="m">--help</span> is fast, execute, print a result, and exit with the appropriate code.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; <span class="m">solve --resume</span>: the data-directory requirement is waived.<br>
A2 &mdash; <span class="m">playbook --clear</span>: the playbook file is deleted; a failure to delete is swallowed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; <span class="m">solve</span> without <span class="m">--resume</span> and without a valid data directory: exit code 2.<br>
E2 &mdash; A run that does not reach completion: exit code 1.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-001, FR-009, FR-018, FR-024, FR-026, FR-032.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Ten commands are available: <span class="m">run</span>, <span class="m">team</span>, <span class="m">solve</span>, <span class="m">sessions</span>, <span class="m">recall</span>, <span class="m">index</span>, <span class="m">mcp-serve</span>, <span class="m">playbook</span>, <span class="m">guardrail-benchmark</span>, <span class="m">serve</span>.<br>
AC2 &mdash; Exit codes are real signals: 0 on success, 1 on incomplete run, 2 on invalid arguments, so chained shell usage behaves like a build step.<br>
AC3 &mdash; The module invocation form works identically to the installed console command.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Implemented.</b> <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the CLI module's own documentation string omits three of its commands (<span class="m">solve</span>, <span class="m">mcp-serve</span>, <span class="m">playbook</span>). Correct it.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.34 FR-032 &mdash; Web Dashboard and Live Streaming</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th>Specification</th></tr>
<tr><td>Description</td><td>The system shall serve a single-page web dashboard that streams the steps of a run live over a WebSocket, lists and displays past sessions, lists and displays search runs with their trees and reports, and displays the current playbook.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Actors</td><td>ML Engineer, Reviewer / Manager.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preconditions</td><td>The server is running and bound to a reachable host and port.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Postconditions</td><td>The requested view is rendered; a run started from the dashboard has executed to completion.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main Flow</td><td>1. On startup, bind the connection manager to the running event loop, subscribe it to all future sessions, and start the broadcast loop.<br>
2. A client connects to the live socket and is added to the active set.<br>
3. When a run is started through the dashboard, it executes <b>inside the dashboard's own process</b>, offloaded to a worker thread so the event loop is not blocked.<br>
4. Each step is bridged from the worker thread into the event loop and broadcast to all connected clients.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Alternative Flow</td><td>A1 &mdash; No clients connected: steps produced during that period are dropped. There is no buffering.<br>
A2 &mdash; A session id is supplied as a prefix rather than a full identifier: it is resolved by prefix match.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>E1 &mdash; Client disconnects: the socket is discarded silently.<br>
E2 &mdash; A corrupt run journal: that run is listed with zero nodes rather than failing the whole listing.<br>
E3 &mdash; Session not found: a 200 response carrying an explanatory <span class="m">error</span> field, not an HTTP error status.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Priority</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>FR-001, FR-006, FR-009, FR-018.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance Criteria</td><td>AC1 &mdash; Seven HTTP routes and one WebSocket route are served (see &sect;8).<br>
AC2 &mdash; A real end-to-end chain works: real server, real WebSocket client, real run request, live steps delivered in order.<br>
AC3 &mdash; A long-running agent run does not block other HTTP requests or the broadcast loop.<br>
AC4 &mdash; The page is fully self-contained with no build step.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation Status</td><td><b>Partial.</b> Manually verified end-to-end during development with a real server and a real WebSocket client, but with no committed test (&sect;16.8). Three known gaps: <span class="todo">(a) runs started in a separate process do not appear in the live feed &mdash; a structural consequence of DC-04, since the session store is a per-process singleton and no broker exists to bridge processes. Completed runs still appear in history, so this is a live-view limitation, not data loss. (b) Every failure returns HTTP 200 with an error field; there are no error status codes. (c) The dashboard has no authentication at all &mdash; see &sect;11 and RISK-03.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>5. Non-Functional Requirements</h1>

<p class="note"><b>Read this before the table.</b> The design material contains almost no quantified non-functional targets. It specifies mechanisms (timeouts, caps, retries, budgets) very precisely and service levels not at all. Every number below is therefore either a <b>measured or coded constant</b> taken from the implementation, or an explicitly flagged <span class="assume">[Assumption]</span>. Nothing here has been agreed with a stakeholder. Treat the assumption rows as a proposal to review, not as a baseline.</p>

<h2>5.1 Performance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-001</td><td>A single node's code execution shall be bounded by a configurable timeout, default 600 s within tree search and 300 s for direct tool execution.</td><td>Coded constant.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-002</td><td>Captured execution output shall be capped at 50,000 characters, preserving head and tail.</td><td>Coded constant.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-003</td><td>Terminal output presented to the review model shall be capped at 6,000 characters.</td><td>Coded constant.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-004</td><td>Conversation context shall be compacted once it exceeds 400,000 characters.</td><td>Coded constant.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-005</td><td>Four parallel drafts shall complete in approximately the wall-clock time of one draft.</td><td>Design claim in the HLD. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; unmeasured. Do not quote externally until benchmarked.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-006</td><td>Dashboard API responses (excluding the run endpoint) shall return within 500 ms for an index of 100 sessions and 50 runs.</td><td><span class="assume">[Assumption]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-007</td><td>A live step shall reach a connected WebSocket client within 250 ms of being recorded.</td><td><span class="assume">[Assumption]</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-008</td><td><span class="m">--help</span> for any CLI command shall return within 1 s.</td><td>Supported by the deliberate lazy-import design; unmeasured.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.2 Scalability</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-009</td><td>Search shall scale across a configurable thread pool, default 1 worker.</td><td>Coded constant.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-010</td><td>The system is designed for single-process, single-user operation. It shall not be assumed to scale horizontally.</td><td>Direct consequence of AS-05 and the per-process singletons. This is a real ceiling, not a tuning parameter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-011</td><td>Dataset size shall be bounded by host memory.</td><td>The dataset registry is an in-process dictionary of dataframes with no spill-to-disk. <span class="assume">[Assumption: ~2 GB practical ceiling.]</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-012</td><td>The session index shall retain the 100 most recent runs; the run archive and journals are unbounded.</td><td>Coded constant / gap. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no retention policy exists for <span class="m">runs/</span>. Disk growth is unmanaged.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.3 Availability and Reliability</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-013</td><td>The search journal shall be written after every node so that killing the process at any point leaves a resumable run.</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-014</td><td>No single failing tool, missing optional library or broken candidate model shall terminate a run.</td><td>Implemented via per-candidate isolation and the errors-as-strings contract.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-015</td><td>Knowledge-store failures shall be non-fatal and shall degrade to an empty result.</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-016</td><td>A failing dashboard subscriber shall not affect the agent run.</td><td>Implemented and verified.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-017</td><td>Service availability target.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not applicable to the current local-tool deployment model. If Swarn is ever hosted, an availability target must be defined.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-018</td><td>A run interrupted before completion loses its persisted trace and summary (though not its search journal).</td><td>Known characteristic. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; decide whether incremental trace persistence is required.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.4 Maintainability and Extensibility</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-019</td><td>New capability shall be added by registering new tools or by supplying optional configuration, never by modifying the agent loop's control flow.</td><td>DC-01. Sustained across sixteen development phases.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-020</td><td>Every tool shall return errors as strings and shall never raise into the loop.</td><td>DC-02. This single contract is what lets one correction mechanism serve all 45 tools.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-021</td><td>Role prompts shall be constructed at import time from one shared core, so they cannot drift out of sync.</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-022</td><td>The core test suite shall run offline with zero third-party dependencies.</td><td>DC-05. 58 tests across 9 modules.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-023</td><td>Optional heavy dependencies shall be imported lazily behind a cheap safety check.</td><td>DC-06.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-024</td><td>Code coverage target.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not defined. <span class="assume">[Assumption: 70% line coverage on the core loop, search engine and tool registry.]</span></span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.5 Security, Authentication and Authorization</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-025</td><td>Agent file I/O shall be confined to the workspace directory by an explicit path guard rejecting traversal.</td><td>Implemented. DC-07.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-026</td><td>Generated code shall execute in a container with bounded memory (2 GB) and CPU (2 cores) when Docker is available.</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-027</td><td>Tool output shall be scanned for prompt-injection patterns and annotated, never silently stripped.</td><td>Implemented (FR-029).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-028</td><td>Authentication.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; <b>none exists</b>. Not on the dashboard, not on the MCP server, not on the CLI. See &sect;11 and RISK-03. This is the single largest gap in the specification.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-029</td><td>Authorization.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; <b>none exists</b> at the system boundary. Per-role tool restriction (FR-024) is an internal design-quality mechanism, not a security control, and must not be presented as one.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-030</td><td>Secrets shall be supplied by environment variable and never committed.</td><td>Partially implemented; see &sect;11.4 for the hard-coded default endpoint issue.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.6 Logging and Monitoring</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-031</td><td>Every run shall persist a complete machine-readable trace and a human-readable summary.</td><td>Implemented (FR-006).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-032</td><td>Every language-model call and tool call shall be traceable via OpenTelemetry spans when tracing is enabled.</td><td>Implemented (FR-030).</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-033</td><td>Alerting.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no alerting exists. Nothing notifies anyone of a failed or runaway run.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-034</td><td>Log redaction of secrets or personal data.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not implemented. Traces record raw tool inputs and outputs, which may include data content.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.7 Accessibility, Localization and Usability</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-035</td><td>Accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA on the dashboard).</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not addressed. The dashboard has not been audited for contrast, keyboard navigation or screen-reader support.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-036</td><td>Localization.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not implemented. All interface text is English and hard-coded. <span class="assume">[Assumption: English-only is acceptable for v1.]</span></span></td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-037</td><td>Error messages shall be actionable, naming the likely cause and the next step, not merely reporting failure.</td><td>Implemented as a consistent pattern throughout, e.g. naming the missing package to install or the likely network restriction.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-038</td><td>The system shall be transparent about what it did: every run produces a replayable trace and, for searches, an ASCII solution tree.</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-039</td><td>The dashboard shall require no build step and shall be a single self-contained page.</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.8 Compliance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-040</td><td>Regulatory compliance obligations (GDPR, DPDP Act, ISO 27001, SOC 2 or sector-specific).</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; none identified in the design material. This is a question for the manager, not something to infer. If Swarn will process customer or personal data, this section becomes substantial and blocking.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-041</td><td>Third-party licence compliance for the dependency set.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no licence audit has been performed.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-042</td><td>Data residency for the language-model endpoint.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the test endpoint is a third-party hosted deployment. Task descriptions and data previews are transmitted to it. Residency and processing terms are unspecified.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.9 Backup and Recovery</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-043</td><td>A killed search run shall be resumable from its journal.</td><td>Implemented (FR-014).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-044</td><td>Backup of <span class="m">runs/</span>, <span class="m">sessions/</span>, <span class="m">knowledge/</span> and <span class="m">.chroma/</span>.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no backup mechanism exists. All state is local filesystem. <span class="assume">[Assumption: the operator's own machine backup is considered sufficient for v1.]</span></span></td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-045</td><td>Recovery from a corrupt journal.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; undefined (see FR-014 E2).</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.10 Rate Limiting, Caching, Timeouts and Concurrency</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:44%">Requirement</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-046</td><td>Rate limiting on inbound interfaces.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; none. The dashboard's run endpoint can be invoked without limit.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-047</td><td>Outbound rate limiting shall be handled reactively: rate-limit responses are treated as retryable with backoff.</td><td>Implemented (FR-004). There is no proactive request pacing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-048</td><td>Language-model clients shall be cached per endpoint identity for the process lifetime.</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-049</td><td>Response caching.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no model-response cache exists. Identical prompts are re-billed. A prompt cache would reduce cost on repeated drafts.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-050</td><td>Every outbound HTTP call shall have an explicit timeout.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; <b>not met.</b> No per-request timeout is configured on the model client. Because "timeout" is a retryable marker, a hung request could consume all five attempts before failing. Fix this.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>NFR-051</td><td>Concurrency shall be bounded by the configured worker count, and shared state shall be protected.</td><td>Implemented: reservation-aware scheduling in search; lock-protected task registry in the MCP server; one owning task per MCP connection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFR-052</td><td>Two Swarn processes shall not be assumed to share any state.</td><td>Implemented characteristic, and the direct cause of the dashboard live-view limitation.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>6. System Architecture Summary</h1>

<h2>6.1 Architecture Layers</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Layer</th><th style="width:34%">Responsibility</th><th>Principal modules</th></tr>
<tr><td>Interface</td><td>Accept work from humans and machines; present results.</td><td><span class="m">main.py</span> (REPL), <span class="m">cli.py</span>, <span class="m">dashboard.py</span>, <span class="m">mcp_server.py</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Orchestration</td><td>Decide what to do next and drive it to completion.</td><td><span class="m">agent_loop.py</span>, <span class="m">orchestrator.py</span>, <span class="m">roles.py</span>, <span class="m">search/agent.py</span>, <span class="m">search/runner.py</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Policy &amp; safety</td><td>Constrain, classify and annotate without deciding.</td><td><span class="m">self_correction.py</span>, <span class="m">doom_loop.py</span>, <span class="m">search/static_check.py</span>, <span class="m">observability.py</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Capability</td><td>The actual work: one registry of callable tools.</td><td><span class="m">tools.py</span> (all 45 registrations) delegating to 13 implementing modules</td></tr>
<tr><td>Domain (ML)</td><td>The data-to-deployment assembly line.</td><td><span class="m">data_pipeline.py</span>, <span class="m">feature_engineering.py</span>, <span class="m">model_training.py</span>, <span class="m">evaluation.py</span>, <span class="m">deployment.py</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Knowledge &amp; memory</td><td>What happened, and what was learned from it.</td><td><span class="m">memory.py</span>, <span class="m">knowledge.py</span>, <span class="m">context_engine.py</span>, <span class="m">multimodal_rag.py</span>, <span class="m">search/journal.py</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Integration</td><td>Talk to the model and to the outside world.</td><td><span class="m">llm/</span>, <span class="m">mcp_integration.py</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Execution</td><td>Run untrusted generated code safely.</td><td><span class="m">execution.py</span>, <span class="m">sandbox.py</span> (compatibility shim)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>6.2 Component Responsibilities</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Component</th><th style="width:38%">Responsibility</th><th>Key design decision</th></tr>
<tr><td>Agent loop</td><td>Drive reason&rarr;act&rarr;observe to completion.</td><td>Never modified to add capability; recomputes tool definitions every iteration so mid-run registrations are visible.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Tool registry</td><td>Single source of truth for callable capability.</td><td>A plain dictionary. Dynamic MCP visibility is granted only to roles already trusted to manage connections.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Search engine</td><td>Systematic experimentation over a solution tree.</td><td>Policy is explicit and testable in isolation from model intelligence.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Journal</td><td>Crash-safe record of the solution tree.</td><td>Saved after every node, which is what makes resume possible at all.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reviewer call</td><td>Turn execution output into a structured verdict.</td><td>Forced tool call at low temperature; the script's printed metric overrides the reviewer on conflict &mdash; regex plus reviewer, not judgement alone.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Knowledge store</td><td>Learn across runs.</td><td>Hard-capped playbook with oldest-first eviction. Bounded memory by construction, not by hope.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Orchestrator</td><td>Coordinate four roles.</td><td>Blackboard, not shared chat history &mdash; roles pass forward a written summary the way a team passes a ticket.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>MCP integration</td><td>Bridge a synchronous codebase to an async-only SDK.</td><td>One persistent background event loop; one dedicated owning task per server connection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Execution backend</td><td>Run code safely and portably.</td><td>Docker for isolation, subprocess for universality; auto-detected, never a hard requirement.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Guardrails</td><td>Surface manipulation attempts in tool output.</td><td>Annotate, never strip. The model is the party best positioned to resist an attack it can see.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>6.3 Communication</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Path</th><th style="width:18%">Mechanism</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Interface &rarr; orchestration</td><td>In-process function call</td><td>All four front ends call the same loop or engine.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Orchestration &rarr; model</td><td>HTTPS, OpenAI-compatible</td><td>Single endpoint, 5 retries, capped exponential backoff.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Orchestration &rarr; execution</td><td>Docker SDK or subprocess</td><td>Bounded by timeout; output truncated.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Registry &rarr; external MCP server</td><td>stdio, via a queue and a per-server owning task</td><td>Synchronous caller blocks on a future.</td></tr>
<tr><td>MCP client &rarr; Swarn</td><td>stdio</td><td>Four tools; asynchronous submit-and-poll.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Agent run &rarr; dashboard</td><td>In-process callback bridged to the event loop, then WebSocket</td><td>Only works within one process (DC-04).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Runtime &rarr; observability backend</td><td>OpenTelemetry export</td><td>Optional; console exporter when no collector is configured.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>6.4 External Systems</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">System</th><th style="width:16%">Direction</th><th>Interface and criticality</th></tr>
<tr><td>Deployed LLM endpoint</td><td>Outbound</td><td>HTTPS, OpenAI-compatible. <b>Single point of failure</b> &mdash; nothing works without it.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Docker daemon</td><td>Outbound, local</td><td>Docker SDK. Optional; graceful fallback.</td></tr>
<tr><td>External MCP servers</td><td>Outbound</td><td>stdio subprocess. Optional.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>MCP clients (IDEs, agents)</td><td>Inbound</td><td>stdio. Optional.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Model hub (embeddings, base models)</td><td>Outbound</td><td>HTTPS on first use. Optional; clean failure when restricted.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Cloud object storage / SQL databases</td><td>Outbound</td><td>Via optional drivers, when a data connector is used.</td></tr>
<tr><td>OpenTelemetry collector</td><td>Outbound</td><td>Optional.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>7. Data Requirements</h1>

<h2>7.1 Overview</h2>
<p>Swarn has no central relational database. It uses four distinct persistence mechanisms, each chosen for a different access pattern, plus two in-memory registries that live only for the process lifetime.</p>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Store</th><th style="width:18%">Technology</th><th style="width:24%">Location</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td>Run archive</td><td>SQLite with FTS5</td><td><span class="m">knowledge/runs.db</span></td><td>Full-text retrieval of similar past runs.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Playbook</td><td>Markdown file</td><td><span class="m">knowledge/playbook.md</span></td><td>Bounded, human-readable learned lessons.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Session traces</td><td>JSON + Markdown</td><td><span class="m">sessions/&lt;uuid&gt;/</span></td><td>Replayable record of one agent run.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Solution journals</td><td>JSON</td><td><span class="m">runs/&lt;run_id&gt;/journal.json</span></td><td>Crash-safe solution tree.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Semantic index</td><td>ChromaDB (embedded)</td><td><span class="m">.chroma/</span></td><td>Vector search over code and documents.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Dataset registry</td><td>In-memory dictionary</td><td>Process memory</td><td>Named dataframes for the ML pipeline.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Model registry</td><td>In-memory dictionary</td><td>Process memory</td><td>Trained artifacts with metrics and held-out splits.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>MCP task registry</td><td>In-memory dictionary</td><td>Process memory</td><td>Status of tasks submitted over MCP.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7.2 Entities</h2>

<h3>7.2.1 Run (full-text archive)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Column</th><th style="width:16%">Indexed</th><th style="width:18%">Truncation</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>run_id</td><td>Yes (FTS5)</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Identifier of the archived run.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>task</td><td>Yes (FTS5)</td><td>4,000 chars</td><td>The task description.</td></tr>
<tr><td>summary</td><td>Yes (FTS5)</td><td>4,000 chars</td><td>Outcome summary.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>code</td><td>Yes (FTS5)</td><td>20,000 chars</td><td>The winning solution's source.</td></tr>
<tr><td>metric</td><td>No</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Best metric, stored as text; empty when absent.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>ts</td><td>No</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Unix timestamp.</td></tr>
</table>
<p><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; this table has no primary key, no unique constraint and no secondary index. Re-indexing an existing run id silently creates duplicate rows. Add uniqueness on <span class="m">run_id</span> or convert to an upsert.</span></p>

<h3>7.2.2 Session</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Field</th><th style="width:16%">Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>id</td><td>string (UUID4)</td><td>Primary identifier; also the directory name.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>task</td><td>string</td><td>The submitted task.</td></tr>
<tr><td>model</td><td>string</td><td>Model label (display only; routing is fixed).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>started_at / ended_at</td><td>float / nullable float</td><td>Unix timestamps.</td></tr>
<tr><td>steps</td><td>list of Step</td><td>Ordered record of the run.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>outcome</td><td>nullable string</td><td>The code produces exactly four values: <span class="m">complete</span>, <span class="m">no_tool_use</span>, <span class="m">max_iterations</span>, <span class="m">max_corrections</span>. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the module documentation is inconsistent with this in both directions: it lists <span class="m">error</span>, which no code path produces, and omits <span class="m">no_tool_use</span>, which is produced. Reconcile and make this a closed enumeration in code.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>summary</td><td>nullable string</td><td>Supplied by the completion tool.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>corrections</td><td>integer</td><td>Count of correction events.</td></tr>
<tr><td>on_step</td><td>list of callables</td><td>Live subscribers. Excluded from persistence and comparison.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>7.2.3 Step</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Field</th><th style="width:16%">Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>kind</td><td>enum</td><td>One of: plan, tool_call, tool_result, correction, complete, error.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>timestamp</td><td>float</td><td>Unix time. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; serialised as <span class="m">time</span> on disk but as <span class="m">timestamp</span> over the WebSocket; the dashboard reads the wrong one from disk. Unify.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>data</td><td>object</td><td>Kind-specific payload (tool name, input, result, error kind, attempt number).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>7.2.4 Node (solution attempt)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Field</th><th style="width:16%">Type</th><th style="width:14%">Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>id</td><td>string</td><td>random 8 hex</td><td>Node identifier.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>parent_id</td><td>nullable string</td><td>null</td><td>Null for root drafts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>children</td><td>list of string</td><td>[]</td><td>Child node ids.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>step</td><td>integer</td><td>assigned on append</td><td>Position in the run.</td></tr>
<tr><td>stage</td><td>string</td><td>draft</td><td>draft / debug / improve.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>plan, code</td><td>string</td><td>""</td><td>The intent and the generated source.</td></tr>
<tr><td>term_out</td><td>string</td><td>""</td><td>Captured execution output.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>exec_time, exit_code, timed_out</td><td>float / int / bool</td><td>0.0 / 0 / false</td><td>Execution result.</td></tr>
<tr><td>analysis</td><td>string</td><td>""</td><td>Reviewer's summary.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>metric</td><td>nullable float</td><td>null</td><td>Reviewed score.</td></tr>
<tr><td>lower_is_better</td><td>bool</td><td>false</td><td>Metric direction.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>is_buggy</td><td>bool</td><td><b>true</b></td><td>Pessimistic default &mdash; a node is buggy until a review proves otherwise.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ctime</td><td>float</td><td>now</td><td>Creation time.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>7.2.5 Model artifact (in memory)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>model</td><td>The fitted estimator.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>task_type</td><td>regression / binary_classification / multiclass_classification.</td></tr>
<tr><td>metrics</td><td>Metrics computed on the held-out split.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>feature_columns, target_col</td><td>Contract for downstream evaluation and deployment.</td></tr>
<tr><td>X_test, y_test</td><td>The held-out split, retained deliberately so evaluation scores the exact rows the reported metrics came from.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7.3 Relationships</h2>
<pre>
Session 1 ──── N Step

SearchRun 1 ──── 1 Journal 1 ──── N Node
                                   │
                                   └── parent_id ──► Node   (self-referencing tree)

SearchRun 1 ──── 0..1 ArchivedRun   (written once on reflection)
SearchRun 1 ──── 0..N PlaybookLesson (at most 5 accepted per run)

Dataset 1 ──── N ModelArtifact 1 ──── 0..1 DeploymentPackage
</pre>

<h2>7.4 Constraints</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:40%">Constraint</th><th>Enforcement</th></tr>
<tr><td>DR-01</td><td>Session id is unique.</td><td>UUID4 generation. Index upsert removes any prior entry with the same id.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DR-02</td><td>Node id is unique within a journal.</td><td>Random 8-hex generation and an id map. <span class="assume">[Assumption: collision probability is acceptable at this run size.]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>DR-03</td><td>A node is <i>good</i> only if not buggy and metric is non-null.</td><td>Derived property; not overridable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DR-04</td><td>Playbook total size &le; 6,000 characters; each lesson &le; 300 characters.</td><td>Truncation plus oldest-first eviction.</td></tr>
<tr><td>DR-05</td><td>Session index &le; 100 entries.</td><td>List truncation on upsert.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DR-06</td><td>Run id uniqueness in the archive.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not enforced (see &sect;7.2.1).</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>DR-07</td><td>Agent-written files stay inside the workspace.</td><td>Path guard rejecting resolved paths outside the root.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7.5 Indexes</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Store</th><th style="width:24%">Index</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Run archive</td><td>FTS5 over run_id, task, summary, code</td><td>metric and ts are explicitly unindexed. Ranked by FTS relevance, limited to 3 results.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Run archive</td><td>Secondary indexes</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; none. Time-range or metric-range queries would require a full scan.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Semantic index</td><td>Vector nearest-neighbour over 384-dimension embeddings</td><td>Embedded store, file-backed, no server.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Session index</td><td>File-based list, most-recent-first</td><td>Not a database index; a bounded JSON list.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Journal</td><td>In-memory id map, rebuilt on load</td><td>Not persisted separately.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7.6 Data Validation</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:34%">Rule</th><th>Behaviour on violation</th></tr>
<tr><td>DV-01</td><td>Dataset quality: dtypes, per-column nulls, exact duplicate rows, numeric outliers beyond 3 standard deviations, optional schema check.</td><td>Advisory report only. Never raises, never blocks.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DV-02</td><td>Fine-tune examples must each contain a prompt and a completion field, both non-empty.</td><td>Rejected before any training starts, naming the row index and the keys actually found.</td></tr>
<tr><td>DV-03</td><td>Generated solution code must parse and must print a final metric.</td><td>Scored buggy by the static gate with no sandbox execution consumed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DV-04</td><td>API request bodies must match the declared schema.</td><td>Framework-level validation returns 422.</td></tr>
<tr><td>DV-05</td><td>File paths must resolve inside the workspace.</td><td>Rejected with an error string.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>DV-06</td><td>Input data content validation (PII, injection in data, schema drift over time).</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not implemented.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7.7 Data Retention</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:24%">Data</th><th style="width:22%">Retention</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Session index</td><td>100 most recent runs</td><td>Enforced.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Session trace directories</td><td>Unbounded</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; directories are never pruned even after they leave the index, so they become unreachable through the interface but continue to consume disk.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Playbook</td><td>6,000 characters, oldest-first eviction</td><td>Enforced.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Run archive</td><td>Unbounded</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no retention policy.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Run directories (journals, workspaces, data copies)</td><td>Unbounded</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; each run copies the input data into its workspace by default. Disk growth is linear in runs &times; dataset size and is the fastest-growing store. This needs a policy.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Semantic index</td><td>Unbounded</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no eviction or re-index policy.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>MCP task records</td><td>Process lifetime</td><td>Lost on restart; never pruned within a session.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>8. API Requirements</h1>

<p class="legend">Two API surfaces exist: the dashboard HTTP/WebSocket API (API-001 to API-008) and the MCP tool API (API-009 to API-012). Neither is authenticated. All dashboard base paths are relative to <span class="m">http://&lt;host&gt;:&lt;port&gt;</span>, default <span class="m">127.0.0.1:8420</span>.</p>

<h2>8.1 API-001 &mdash; Serve Dashboard Page</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Serve the single-page dashboard.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Method / Endpoint</td><td><span class="m">GET /</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Headers</td><td>None required.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Authentication</td><td><span class="todo">None.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Request</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Response</td><td><span class="m">text/html</span> &mdash; a self-contained page with no external build assets.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Status codes</td><td>200</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Validation / Errors</td><td>None.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.2 API-002 &mdash; List Sessions</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>List recent agent sessions.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Method / Endpoint</td><td><span class="m">GET /api/sessions?limit=&lt;int&gt;</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication</td><td><span class="todo">None.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Request</td><td>Query parameter <span class="m">limit</span>, integer, default 20.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Response</td><td><span class="m">{"sessions": [{id, task, model, outcome, duration_s, tool_calls, corrections, started_at}]}</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Status codes</td><td>200; 422 when <span class="m">limit</span> is not an integer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Validation</td><td><span class="m">limit</span> must be an integer. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no upper bound is enforced.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Errors</td><td>None beyond validation.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.3 API-003 &mdash; Get Session Detail</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Retrieve a completed session's full trace.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Method / Endpoint</td><td><span class="m">GET /api/sessions/{session_id}</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication</td><td><span class="todo">None.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Request</td><td>Path parameter: full UUID or a unique prefix.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Response (found)</td><td>The complete trace object: id, task, model, started_at, ended_at, outcome, summary, corrections, duration_s, tool_counts, steps.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Response (not found)</td><td><span class="m">{"error": "No completed session found matching '&lt;id&gt;'. It may still be running — watch /ws/live instead."}</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Status codes</td><td>200 in both cases.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Validation / Errors</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not-found should be 404. Returning 200 with an error body means an HTTP client cannot distinguish success from failure without parsing the payload.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.4 API-004 &mdash; List Search Runs</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>List search runs with node counts and best metrics.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Method / Endpoint</td><td><span class="m">GET /api/runs?limit=&lt;int&gt;</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication</td><td><span class="todo">None.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Request</td><td>Query parameter <span class="m">limit</span>, integer, default 50.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Response</td><td><span class="m">{"runs": [{run_id, nodes, best_metric}]}</span>, most recent first.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Status codes</td><td>200; 422 on invalid <span class="m">limit</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Validation / Errors</td><td>A corrupt journal yields <span class="m">nodes: 0, best_metric: null</span> for that run rather than failing the listing. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the corruption is silently swallowed and not logged.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.5 API-005 &mdash; Get Search Run Detail</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Retrieve one run's full solution tree and report.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Method / Endpoint</td><td><span class="m">GET /api/runs/{run_id}</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication</td><td><span class="todo">None.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Response (found)</td><td><span class="m">{run_id, journal?, report_markdown?}</span> &mdash; the optional fields appear only when the corresponding files exist.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Response (not found)</td><td><span class="m">{"error": "no run '&lt;id&gt;'"}</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Status codes</td><td>200 in both cases.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Validation / Errors</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; should be 404 on not-found. Also, the run id is used to build a filesystem path; confirm the path-traversal guard covers this parameter.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.6 API-006 &mdash; Get Playbook</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Return the current cross-run playbook.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Method / Endpoint</td><td><span class="m">GET /api/playbook</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication</td><td><span class="todo">None.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Response</td><td><span class="m">{"playbook": "&lt;markdown&gt;"}</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Status codes</td><td>200</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Validation / Errors</td><td>Any store failure yields an empty string rather than an error. Consistent with the best-effort knowledge contract, but indistinguishable from a genuinely empty playbook.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.7 API-007 &mdash; Start Agent Run</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Execute an agent task inside the dashboard process so its steps can be streamed live.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Method / Endpoint</td><td><span class="m">POST /api/run</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Headers</td><td><span class="m">Content-Type: application/json</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Authentication</td><td><span class="todo">None. This endpoint executes arbitrary agent tasks, including shell commands, for any caller who can reach the port. This is the highest-severity item in this document &mdash; see RISK-03.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Request</td><td><span class="m">{"task": "&lt;string, required&gt;", "model": "&lt;string, optional&gt;"}</span>. The model field is display-only for every value <b>except</b> <span class="m">mock</span> / <span class="m">mock:&lt;name&gt;</span>, which switches the run to the offline scripted client (FR-004 A1). <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; this is an unauthenticated remote switch into a test mode; combined with the absence of authentication it is a defect worth closing.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Response</td><td><span class="m">{"outcome": "&lt;string&gt;", "summary": "&lt;string|null&gt;", "session_id": "&lt;uuid&gt;"}</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Status codes</td><td>200; 422 on an invalid body.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Validation</td><td><span class="m">task</span> is required and must be a string. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no length limit, no content policy, no rate limit.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Errors</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the response carries no error field. An LLM failure propagates as an unhandled framework error (500) rather than a structured response.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Behaviour note</td><td>The request blocks for the entire duration of the run, which may be many minutes. There is no job-id-and-poll variant. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; clients will hit their own HTTP timeouts. Consider an asynchronous submission pattern like the MCP server already uses.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.8 API-008 &mdash; Live Step Stream</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Stream steps of runs executing in this process to connected clients.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Method / Endpoint</td><td><span class="m">WebSocket /ws/live</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication</td><td><span class="todo">None.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Server &rarr; client message</td><td><span class="m">{"session_id": str, "task": str (first 120 chars), "kind": str, "timestamp": float, "data": object}</span>, JSON text frame.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Client &rarr; server message</td><td>No format is defined or parsed. Inbound frames are read only to detect disconnection.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Status codes</td><td>Standard WebSocket close codes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Validation / Errors</td><td>Disconnection removes the socket silently. Steps produced while no client is connected are dropped; there is no buffering or replay.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Constraint</td><td>Only runs executing inside this process are visible (DC-04, &sect;10.5).</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.9 API-009 &mdash; MCP: Submit Task</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Submit a task for asynchronous execution.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Transport</td><td>MCP over stdio. Tool name <span class="m">swarn_submit_task</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Inherited from the stdio process boundary. No in-protocol authentication.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Request</td><td><span class="m">task</span> (string, required); <span class="m">data_dir</span> (string, default ""); <span class="m">steps</span> (integer, default 12); <span class="m">mode</span> (string, default "auto", one of solve / agent / auto); <span class="m">model</span> (string, default "").</td></tr>
<tr><td>Response</td><td>String: <span class="m">Task &lt;id&gt; submitted (mode=&lt;mode&gt;). Poll with swarn_task_status(task_id="&lt;id&gt;").</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Validation</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no validation that <span class="m">data_dir</span> exists, that <span class="m">steps</span> is positive, or that <span class="m">mode</span> is one of the three permitted values. An unrecognised mode falls through to agent mode silently.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Errors</td><td>Failures surface later through the status tool, not at submission.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Note</td><td>The <span class="m">model</span> parameter's documentation advertises provider-prefixed specs that the router ignores. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; correct the documentation.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.10 API-010 &mdash; MCP: Task Status</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Poll one task.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Transport</td><td>MCP tool <span class="m">swarn_task_status</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Request</td><td><span class="m">task_id</span> (string, required).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Response</td><td>Line 1: <span class="m">Task &lt;id&gt;: &lt;status&gt; (&lt;elapsed&gt;s, mode=&lt;mode&gt;)</span>. Optionally a latest-progress line. When not running, the result text is appended.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Status values</td><td><span class="m">running</span> | <span class="m">complete</span> | <span class="m">failed</span>.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Errors</td><td>Unknown id: <span class="m">Unknown task id: &lt;id&gt;</span>.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.11 API-011 &mdash; MCP: Get Messages</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Retrieve a task's transcript and final output.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Transport</td><td>MCP tool <span class="m">swarn_get_messages</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Request</td><td><span class="m">task_id</span> (string, required).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Response</td><td>Header line, the task text, the most recent 100 messages, then a result section.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Errors</td><td>Unknown id: <span class="m">Unknown task id: &lt;id&gt;</span>.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Note</td><td>Agent-mode transcripts retain at most the last 200 captured output lines. Earlier output is not recoverable through this API.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.12 API-012 &mdash; MCP: List Tasks</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Attribute</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>List all tasks submitted in this server session.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Transport</td><td>MCP tool <span class="m">swarn_list_tasks</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Request</td><td>None.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Response</td><td>One line per task: <span class="m">&lt;id&gt; · &lt;status&gt; · [&lt;mode&gt;] &lt;task, first 80 chars&gt;</span>. Empty: <span class="m">No tasks submitted yet.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Errors</td><td>None.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Note</td><td>The list is never pruned and does not survive a restart.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.13 Cross-cutting API observations</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th>Observation</th></tr>
<tr><td>API-X1</td><td><span class="todo">No endpoint on either surface is authenticated.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>API-X2</td><td><span class="todo">The HTTP API never returns 4xx or 5xx for application-level failure. Every failure is a 200 with an error field, which defeats standard client error handling, retries and monitoring.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>API-X3</td><td><span class="todo">No versioning scheme. The paths are <span class="m">/api/...</span> with no version segment, so any breaking change breaks every client silently.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>API-X4</td><td><span class="todo">No CORS configuration, no rate limiting, no request size limits.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>API-X5</td><td>The MCP surface uses an asynchronous submit-and-poll pattern; the HTTP surface uses a blocking pattern for the equivalent operation. These should converge.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>9. Business Rules</h1>

<p class="legend">Each rule below is derived from an explicit statement or an enforced behaviour in the design material or the implementation. No rule has been invented. Where a rule's <i>rationale</i> is stated in the source, it is reproduced, because several of these rules exist specifically to prevent a class of defect that has already occurred once.</p>

<table>
<tr><th style="width:10%">ID</th><th style="width:40%">Rule</th><th>Rationale / enforcement</th></tr>
<tr><td>BR-001</td><td>The tool registry is the only thing that grows. The loop that drives it does not change.</td><td>Sustained across sixteen phases. Prevents the control flow from accumulating special cases.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-002</td><td>A tool must return errors as strings and must never raise into the loop.</td><td>Enables one correction mechanism to serve all 45 tools without per-tool glue.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-003</td><td>Task completion is signalled by calling a tool, not by special-case logic outside the dispatch path.</td><td>Finishing is just another tool call the loop recognises by name.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-004</td><td>Error <i>detection</i> is deterministic code; deciding the <i>fix</i> is the model's judgement.</td><td>The correction policy classifies and hints; it never auto-retries or auto-fixes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-005</td><td>The system aborts after three consecutive tool errors with no success in between.</td><td>One fixable mistake is normal; an unbroken streak means stop and surface the problem rather than spend more model calls.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-006</td><td>A solution node is buggy until a review proves otherwise.</td><td>Pessimistic default. An unparseable or failed review can never promote a node.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-007</td><td>When a script's own printed metric and the reviewer's metric disagree, the printed metric wins.</td><td>Regex plus reviewer, not judgement alone.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-008</td><td>An ambiguous review verdict &mdash; containing neither an approval nor a rejection keyword &mdash; is treated as a rejection.</td><td>Rejection keywords are checked first and the function falls through to rejection. Failing safe matters more than failing convenient.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-009</td><td>On rejection, work routes back to the Coder, never to the Planner.</td><td>Re-planning from scratch over an implementation issue discards a good plan.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-010</td><td>A role that cannot write files cannot be given write tools.</td><td>If the Reviewer could edit, "review" would be a second coding pass and would provide no independent check. The allow-lists are what make a role's judgement mean something.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-011</td><td>Roles exchange written summaries, never a shared chat history.</td><td>Modelled on how a team passes forward a ticket, not a transcript.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-012</td><td>Dynamic MCP tool visibility is granted only to roles whose static allow-list already contains the connection-management tool.</td><td>Connecting a server must not silently widen what an untrusted role can do.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-013</td><td>Profiling suggests; engineering acts. Nothing is dropped or transformed without an explicit instruction.</td><td>Applies BR-004 to feature engineering.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-014</td><td>A fitted transformer must be retained so the identical transform can be reapplied without re-fitting.</td><td>Re-fitting on a test set leaks test information into the transform. The design makes this mistake structurally harder.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-015</td><td>Evaluation must score the exact held-out rows that produced the reported training metrics.</td><td>Re-splitting at evaluation time silently produces a different, possibly easier or harder, test set.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-016</td><td>One failing training candidate must not fail the leaderboard.</td><td>Per-candidate isolation. Demonstrated by a real broken install during development.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-017</td><td>Data validation is advisory. It never raises and never blocks.</td><td>It always returns a report, including when everything is clean.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-018</td><td>Flagged injection content is annotated, never stripped or blocked.</td><td>A real injection attempt is exactly what the model needs to see in order to recognise and refuse it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-019</td><td>Self-correction assessment runs on the raw result <i>before</i> the guardrail scan, never after.</td><td>Prepending a banner first stops the string starting with an error prefix and silently defeats error detection. Two independent enrichment layers are not automatically composable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-020</td><td>Knowledge operations are best-effort and must never fail a run.</td><td>Every database path swallows its error and returns empty.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-021</td><td>The playbook is hard-capped and evicted oldest-first.</td><td>Bounded memory by construction rather than by hope.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-022</td><td>Quantised fine-tuning must not silently fall back to unquantised when no GPU is present.</td><td>The memory/compute trade-off is a consequential decision the caller must make explicitly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-023</td><td>An expensive or risky import must be preceded by a cheap safety check.</td><td>Importing a deep-learning framework has side effects that crash in a broken environment even when nothing from it is used.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-024</td><td>Generated code must be compiled, not merely inspected, before it is trusted.</td><td>Indentation defects at template splice points are invisible on reading and only appear on compilation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-025</td><td>All language-model traffic routes to one deployed endpoint; model specifications are accepted but ignored except the offline mock.</td><td>Deliberate removal of provider choice.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-026</td><td>Agent file operations are confined to the workspace.</td><td>Path guard rejects traversal.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-027</td><td>A subscriber failure must never break an agent run.</td><td>Callback exceptions are swallowed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-028</td><td>Budgets are explicit and user-visible, and exhausting one is a clean stop, not a failure.</td><td>Positioned in the HLD as transparency, in contrast to opaque credit systems.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BR-029</td><td>The journal is saved after every node.</td><td>This is the only reason resume is possible.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>BR-030</td><td>Two concurrent workers must never debug the same node.</td><td>Reservation-aware scheduling.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>10. User Interface Requirements</h1>

<h2>10.1 Interfaces provided</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:22%">Interface</th><th style="width:20%">Entry point</th><th>Primary use</th></tr>
<tr><td>Interactive REPL</td><td><span class="m">python main.py</span></td><td>Exploratory, conversational use.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Command line</td><td><span class="m">swarn &lt;command&gt;</span></td><td>One-off and scripted use; CI integration.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Web dashboard</td><td><span class="m">swarn serve</span></td><td>Live observation and history browsing.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>MCP</td><td><span class="m">swarn mcp-serve</span></td><td>Machine clients; IDE integration.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>10.2 REPL commands</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Command</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
<tr><td>&lt;natural language&gt;</td><td>Single agent, full toolset.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">team &lt;task&gt;</span></td><td>Multi-agent pipeline.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">report</span></td><td>Full report of the last team run.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">history [n]</span></td><td>Recent sessions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">recall &lt;session_id&gt;</span></td><td>A past session's full tool-call log.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">index &lt;path&gt;</span></td><td>Index a directory for semantic search.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">guardrails</span></td><td>Injection patterns flagged this session.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">clear</span></td><td>Empty the workspace.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">exit</span></td><td>Quit.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>10.3 CLI commands</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:18%">Command</th><th style="width:40%">Options (default)</th><th>Exit codes</th></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">run</span></td><td><span class="m">task</span> (required); <span class="m">--model</span> (ignored)</td><td>0 complete, 1 otherwise</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">team</span></td><td><span class="m">task</span> (required); <span class="m">--model</span>; <span class="m">--no-tester</span></td><td>0 complete, 1 otherwise</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">solve</span></td><td><span class="m">task</span> (required); <span class="m">--data/-d</span>; <span class="m">--steps/-s</span> (20); <span class="m">--time-limit/-t</span>; <span class="m">--drafts</span> (4); <span class="m">--exec-timeout</span> (600); <span class="m">--workers/-w</span>; <span class="m">--token-budget</span>; <span class="m">--resume</span>; <span class="m">--no-learn</span>; <span class="m">--model/-m</span>, <span class="m">--feedback-model</span> (ignored)</td><td>0 best found, 1 none, 2 invalid arguments</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">sessions</span></td><td><span class="m">--limit/-n</span> (10)</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">recall</span></td><td><span class="m">session_id</span> (required)</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">index</span></td><td><span class="m">path</span> (required)</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">mcp-serve</span></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>0 (blocks)</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">playbook</span></td><td><span class="m">--clear</span></td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">guardrail-benchmark</span></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><span class="m">serve</span></td><td><span class="m">--port/-p</span> (8420); <span class="m">--host</span> (127.0.0.1)</td><td>blocks until interrupted</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>10.4 Dashboard pages and navigation</h2>
<p>The dashboard is a single page with no routing. All views are sections of the same document, populated by fetch calls.</p>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:24%">Section</th><th style="width:30%">Content</th><th>Backing API</th></tr>
<tr><td>Run a task</td><td>A single text input and a submit control.</td><td>API-007</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Live feed</td><td>Step-by-step stream of the running task, labelled by step kind.</td><td>API-008</td></tr>
<tr><td>Session history</td><td>List of completed sessions; selecting one opens its full trace.</td><td>API-002, API-003</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Search runs</td><td>List of runs with node count and best metric; selecting one shows the tree and report.</td><td>API-004, API-005</td></tr>
<tr><td>Playbook</td><td>Rendered learned lessons.</td><td>API-006</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>10.5 Forms, validation and messages</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Form / field</th><th style="width:24%">Validation</th><th>Message behaviour</th></tr>
<tr><td>Task input</td><td>Non-empty string, enforced by the API schema.</td><td>An empty submission returns 422. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no client-side validation or inline message is specified.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Session lookup</td><td>Full UUID or unique prefix.</td><td>Not-found returns an explanatory message advising that the session may still be running and to watch the live feed instead. Good message; wrong status code (API-X2).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Run lookup</td><td>Run id.</td><td>Not-found returns a terse <span class="m">no run '&lt;id&gt;'</span>. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; less helpful than the session equivalent; make it consistent.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Live feed with no clients</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Steps are dropped. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the UI does not tell the user that they may have missed steps by connecting late.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Cross-process runs</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>A run started in another terminal never appears in the live feed. This is stated in the documentation but <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; is not surfaced in the interface, where its absence looks like a bug.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>10.6 Responsive behaviour</h2>
<p><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not specified and not implemented. The page is a single self-contained document with no stated breakpoints, no mobile layout and no audited behaviour below desktop widths. <span class="assume">[Assumption: desktop-only is acceptable for v1, since the tool is operated from a development machine.]</span> If a manager or reviewer is expected to check runs from a phone, this becomes a real requirement.</span></p>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>11. Security Requirements</h1>

<p class="note"><b>Summary for the manager, stated plainly.</b> Swarn's <i>internal</i> safety mechanisms are genuinely good: sandboxed execution with resource caps, a workspace path guard, prompt-injection annotation, tool restriction by role, and a correction policy that stops runaway loops. Its <i>perimeter</i> security is absent: there is no authentication, no authorization and no rate limiting on any interface, and one endpoint executes arbitrary tasks. This is defensible for a single-user local tool on a trusted machine (AS-03). It is not defensible for anything shared, and the gap between those two situations is one command-line flag (<span class="m">--host</span>).</p>

<h2>11.1 Authentication</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:32%">Requirement</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-001</td><td>The dashboard shall authenticate every request.</td><td><span class="todo">Not implemented. No dependency, middleware, key or session mechanism exists.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SEC-002</td><td>The MCP server shall authenticate its client.</td><td>Implicitly satisfied while transport is stdio, because the process boundary is the control. <span class="todo">Must be revisited before any network transport is added.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-003</td><td>Outbound calls to the model endpoint shall present an API key.</td><td>Implemented via configuration; defaults to a placeholder value for the unsecured test deployment.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>11.2 Authorization</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:32%">Requirement</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-004</td><td>The system shall distinguish read-only from operating users.</td><td><span class="todo">Not implemented. Anyone who can load the dashboard can start a run.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SEC-005</td><td>Agent capability shall be restricted per role.</td><td>Implemented as a <b>design-quality</b> control (BR-010), not a security control. It constrains the agent's own behaviour; it does not constrain a human caller.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-006</td><td>Agent file access shall be confined to the workspace.</td><td>Implemented (DC-07).</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>11.3 Encryption</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:32%">Requirement</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-007</td><td>Traffic to the model endpoint shall use TLS.</td><td>Implemented (HTTPS base URL).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SEC-008</td><td>The dashboard shall be served over TLS.</td><td><span class="todo">Not implemented. Plain HTTP.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-009</td><td>Data at rest shall be encrypted.</td><td><span class="todo">Not implemented. Traces, journals, run archives and copied datasets are written in plaintext.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>11.4 Secrets and token management</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:32%">Requirement</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-010</td><td>Secrets shall be supplied by environment variable.</td><td>Implemented; a dotenv file is loaded at import.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SEC-011</td><td>No credential or private endpoint shall be hard-coded.</td><td><span class="todo">Partially violated. A live third-party test endpoint URL is hard-coded as the default, and the default API key is a placeholder string. Before production, the default must be removed and configuration made mandatory rather than optional.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-012</td><td>Secrets shall not be written to traces or logs.</td><td><span class="todo">Not verified. Traces record raw tool inputs and outputs; a task or tool argument containing a credential would be persisted in plaintext.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SEC-013</td><td>Token lifecycle (rotation, expiry, revocation).</td><td><span class="todo">Not applicable &mdash; no tokens are issued by the system. Becomes applicable the moment SEC-001 is addressed.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>11.5 Session management</h2>
<p><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; there is no user session concept. "Session" in this system means one agent run, not one authenticated user session. No cookies, no session tokens, no idle timeout, no logout. This entire subsection becomes real work once authentication is added.</span></p>

<h2>11.6 Audit logs</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:32%">Requirement</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-014</td><td>Every agent action shall be recorded with a timestamp.</td><td>Implemented via session traces and, optionally, OTel spans.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SEC-015</td><td>Every guardrail finding shall be recorded and retrievable.</td><td>Implemented for the current session.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-016</td><td>Audit records shall be attributable to an actor.</td><td><span class="todo">Not implemented. Traces record what happened, never who asked for it, because no identity exists.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SEC-017</td><td>Audit records shall be tamper-evident.</td><td><span class="todo">Not implemented. Plain JSON files, writable by anyone with filesystem access.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>11.7 Threat notes</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:24%">Threat</th><th style="width:20%">Current mitigation</th><th>Residual exposure</th></tr>
<tr><td>Arbitrary code execution by the agent</td><td>Container isolation with memory and CPU caps; workspace path guard; timeouts.</td><td>Under the subprocess fallback there is no isolation at all &mdash; code runs on the host with the operator's privileges. This fallback is automatic and silent.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Unauthenticated remote task execution</td><td>Default bind to loopback.</td><td>A single <span class="m">--host 0.0.0.0</span> converts a local tool into an open remote-execution service. Nothing warns the operator.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prompt injection via tool output</td><td>Pattern annotation, on by default.</td><td>The pattern list is small and illustrative. Novel phrasings are not detected.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Data exfiltration to the model endpoint</td><td>None.</td><td>Task text and data previews are transmitted to a third-party endpoint. No redaction, no opt-out, no residency guarantee.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Supply chain (external MCP servers)</td><td>None.</td><td>Connecting a server launches an arbitrary subprocess and registers its tools with descriptions and schemas taken verbatim from that server, which the model then reads and acts on.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>12. Error Handling</h1>

<h2>12.1 Error taxonomy</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:16%">Kind</th><th style="width:38%">Detected by</th><th>Guidance injected</th></tr>
<tr><td>SYNTAX</td><td>Syntax, indentation or tab errors.</td><td>Fix the code structure before re-running.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>IMPORT</td><td>Import errors, missing module messages.</td><td>Install the named package via the install tool.</td></tr>
<tr><td>FILE</td><td>File-not-found, no-such-file, is-a-directory.</td><td>Verify the path; list the directory first.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>TIMEOUT</td><td>"timed out" phrasing.</td><td>Reduce the work or raise the timeout.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RUNTIME</td><td>A traceback marker or one of eight named exceptions with a colon.</td><td>Read the exception and correct the logic.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SHELL</td><td>A non-zero exit-code pattern.</td><td>Inspect the command's output.</td></tr>
<tr><td>GENERIC</td><td>Anything else matching an error signature.</td><td>General corrective guidance.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>12.2 System errors</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:34%">Condition</th><th>Handling</th></tr>
<tr><td>EH-001</td><td>A tool raises any exception.</td><td>Caught at dispatch and returned as an error string naming the tool, the exception type and the message. Never propagates (BR-002).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-002</td><td>Three consecutive tool errors.</td><td>Run aborts with an explanatory error step (BR-005).</td></tr>
<tr><td>EH-003</td><td>Iteration cap reached.</td><td>Run ends with outcome <span class="m">max_iterations</span> and a recorded error step.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-004</td><td>Model call fails all retries.</td><td>A typed error is raised describing the last underlying cause.</td></tr>
<tr><td>EH-005</td><td>Knowledge store failure.</td><td>Swallowed; empty result returned (BR-020).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-006</td><td>Live subscriber raises.</td><td>Swallowed (BR-027).</td></tr>
<tr><td>EH-007</td><td>Corrupt journal during run listing.</td><td>Swallowed; the run is listed with zero nodes. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not logged, so silent corruption is undetectable.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-008</td><td>Unhandled failure inside the HTTP run endpoint.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not handled. Surfaces as a framework 500 with no structured body.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>12.3 Validation errors</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:34%">Condition</th><th>Handling</th></tr>
<tr><td>EH-009</td><td>Malformed API request body.</td><td>Framework validation returns 422 with field-level detail.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-010</td><td>CLI: <span class="m">solve</span> without data and without resume.</td><td>Exit code 2 with a message.</td></tr>
<tr><td>EH-011</td><td>Fine-tune example missing a required field.</td><td>Rejected before training, naming the row index and the keys found.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-012</td><td>Generated code fails the static gate.</td><td>Node scored buggy without consuming an execution.</td></tr>
<tr><td>EH-013</td><td>Path outside the workspace.</td><td>Rejected with an error string.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-014</td><td>MCP submit with an invalid mode or a non-existent data directory.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not validated. An unrecognised mode silently resolves to agent mode.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>12.4 Network errors and retry logic</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">ID</th><th style="width:34%">Condition</th><th>Handling</th></tr>
<tr><td>EH-015</td><td>Transient model-endpoint failure: overload, rate limit, 429, 500, 502, 503, 529, timeout, connection, "temporarily".</td><td>Retry up to 5 attempts with capped exponential backoff (approximately 1, 2, 4, 8 s plus jitter, maximum 30 s).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-016</td><td>Non-transient model-endpoint failure.</td><td>Fail immediately without sleeping.</td></tr>
<tr><td>EH-017</td><td>Model-host download blocked or offline.</td><td>Clean, actionable error string naming the model and the likely cause. Never a raw traceback.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-018</td><td>Docker daemon unreachable.</td><td>Silent fallback to the subprocess backend. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; silent is wrong here; the fallback removes isolation and should be announced.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>EH-019</td><td>Remote MCP tool call exceeds its bridge timeout.</td><td>Error string returned to the loop.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>EH-020</td><td>Hung HTTP request to the model endpoint.</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no request timeout is configured (NFR-050). A hung request could consume all five attempts.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>12.5 Fallback strategy</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Failure</th><th>Fallback</th></tr>
<tr><td>Docker unavailable</td><td>Subprocess execution backend.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>ONNX export ineligible</td><td>Pickle serialisation, with an explanation of why.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schema-validation library absent</td><td>Data validation reports everything else and states the check was skipped.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>One training candidate fails</td><td>That leaderboard row records the failure; other candidates rank normally.</td></tr>
<tr><td>OpenTelemetry SDK absent</td><td>All span operations become no-ops.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Reflection call fails</td><td>The run still completes; no lessons are recorded.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PDF table not visually detected</td><td>The tabular content is still indexed through the prose path.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>No good node in a search</td><td>The run finalises with a report explaining the failure and listing recent failures.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>13. Performance Requirements</h1>

<p class="note"><b>Honest status.</b> This section is the weakest in the document, and the weakness is real rather than editorial. The design material contains implementation limits but no service-level targets, and no load testing has been performed. Every row marked as an assumption is a proposal for the manager to accept, change or reject &mdash; it is not a measurement.</p>

<h2>13.1 Response time</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:34%">Operation</th><th style="width:22%">Target</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>Read-only dashboard API</td><td>&lt; 500 ms p95</td><td><span class="assume">[Assumption]</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Live step delivery</td><td>&lt; 250 ms</td><td><span class="assume">[Assumption]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>CLI help</td><td>&lt; 1 s</td><td>Supported by lazy imports; unmeasured.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Single agent run</td><td>Bounded by 30 iterations, not by time</td><td>Coded constant. Wall time is dominated by model latency and is not controlled.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Search run</td><td>Bounded by step, time and token budgets</td><td>Coded and user-supplied.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Node execution</td><td>600 s default, shrunk to fit a remaining time budget</td><td>Coded constant.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>13.2 Concurrency and throughput</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:34%">Dimension</th><th style="width:22%">Value</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>Concurrent human users</td><td>1</td><td>Consequence of AS-05 and per-process singletons. <span class="assume">[Assumption: single-user is acceptable for v1.]</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Concurrent dashboard viewers</td><td>Not bounded in code</td><td><span class="assume">[Assumption: 10 concurrent WebSocket clients.]</span> Untested.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concurrent search workers</td><td>Configurable, default 1</td><td>Coded constant. <span class="assume">[Assumption: practical ceiling equals available CPU cores, since each worker may run a training job.]</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Concurrent MCP tasks</td><td>Unbounded &mdash; one thread per submission</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; a real defect risk. Nothing caps thread creation; a client submitting many tasks can exhaust host resources.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Throughput</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no throughput requirement is meaningful for a single-user tool. Revisit if hosted.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>13.3 Data and storage performance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:34%">Aspect</th><th>Position</th></tr>
<tr><td>Query performance</td><td>Full-text retrieval is limited to 3 results ranked by relevance over a table with no secondary indexes. Adequate at hundreds of runs; <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; unvalidated at thousands, and duplicate rows (&sect;7.2.1) will degrade it.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Vector search</td><td>Embedded store, no server. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no target for index size or query latency.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Journal I/O</td><td>The whole journal is rewritten after every node. Cost grows with tree size. Acceptable at the default 20 steps; <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; unvalidated at hundreds of nodes.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Dataset size</td><td>Bounded by host memory (NFR-011).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disk growth</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; unmanaged. Each run copies the input data by default, so growth is linear in runs &times; dataset size.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>14. Deployment Requirements</h1>

<h2>14.1 Deployment model</h2>
<p>Swarn is delivered as an installable Python package run on a developer or engineering workstation, or on a single server accessed by one operator. There is no hosted, clustered or multi-tenant deployment model, and the architecture does not currently support one (NFR-010).</p>

<h2>14.2 Installation</h2>
<pre>
python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate     # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
pip install -e .                                  # registers the `swarn` command
cp .env.example .env                              # optional endpoint overrides
swarn solve --help
</pre>
<p>Without the editable install, the module invocation form works identically. Optional extras are installed only if the corresponding capability is required (cloud connectors, ONNX export, multimodal extraction, fine-tuning).</p>

<h2>14.3 Environment configuration</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:32%">Variable</th><th style="width:24%">Default</th><th>Controls</th></tr>
<tr><td>SWARN_DEPLOYED_MODEL</td><td>test model name</td><td>Served model name; also the displayed default.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SWARN_DEPLOYED_BASE_URL</td><td>hard-coded test endpoint</td><td>OpenAI-compatible base URL. <span class="todo">See SEC-011.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>SWARN_DEPLOYED_API_KEY</td><td>placeholder</td><td>Endpoint credential.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SWARN_SANDBOX</td><td>auto-detect</td><td>Force <span class="m">docker</span> or <span class="m">subprocess</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWARN_SANDBOX_IMAGE</td><td>python:3.11-slim</td><td>Container image.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SWARN_EXEC_TIMEOUT</td><td>300</td><td>Per-call execution timeout, seconds.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWARN_SEARCH_WORKERS</td><td>1</td><td>Parallel search workers.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SWARN_KNOWLEDGE_DIR</td><td>&lt;repo&gt;/knowledge</td><td>Playbook and run archive location.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWARN_MAX_ITERATIONS</td><td>30</td><td>ReAct loop iteration cap.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SWARN_CONTEXT_CHAR_BUDGET</td><td>400000</td><td>Context compaction threshold.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWARN_CODE_MODEL / SWARN_FEEDBACK_MODEL</td><td>deployed default</td><td>Display labels; also enable the offline mock client.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SWARN_ENABLE_TRACING</td><td>unset</td><td>Enable OpenTelemetry when set to <span class="m">1</span>. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; missing from the example environment file.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>OTEL_EXPORTER_ENDPOINT</td><td>unset (console)</td><td>Trace collector. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; missing from the example environment file.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>14.4 Environments</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Environment</th><th style="width:26%">Purpose</th><th>Configuration difference</th></tr>
<tr><td>Local development</td><td>Day-to-day use.</td><td>Defaults; loopback bind; Docker if available.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Test / CI</td><td>Automated verification.</td><td>Mock model client; subprocess backend; no network.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Production</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no production environment is defined. At minimum this requires: a designated production model endpoint (SEC-011), authentication (SEC-001), TLS (SEC-008), and a data retention policy (&sect;7.7).</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>14.5 CI/CD</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:24%">Stage</th><th>Requirement</th></tr>
<tr><td>Build</td><td>Install the package and its declared dependencies.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Test</td><td>Run the zero-dependency test suite (58 tests) offline. This must pass with no network access and no third-party test framework.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lint / type check</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no configuration is present in the repository.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Security scan</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no dependency vulnerability scanning is configured.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Package / release</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no release process, versioning scheme or changelog is defined.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Pipeline definition</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no CI configuration file exists in the repository. The test suite is CI-ready by design but is not currently wired to a pipeline.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>14.6 Docker and Kubernetes</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:28%">Aspect</th><th>Position</th></tr>
<tr><td>Docker (execution sandbox)</td><td>Used as an isolation backend for generated code. Containers are constrained to 2 GB memory and 2 CPUs with the workspace bind-mounted, and are recycled after a timeout.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Docker (generated services)</td><td>Deployment packaging generates a container definition for the <i>model service</i> it produces &mdash; deliberately separate from Swarn's own dependencies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Docker (Swarn itself)</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; Swarn is not itself containerised. No image or compose definition exists for the platform.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Kubernetes</td><td><span class="todo">Not applicable. The single-process, singleton-state architecture (NFR-010) makes orchestrated multi-replica deployment meaningless without substantial rework: shared state would need externalising and a broker introduced, which DC-04 currently forbids.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>15. Monitoring and Logging</h1>

<h2>15.1 Metrics</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:30%">Metric</th><th style="width:20%">Source</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tool call duration and success</td><td>OTel span attributes</td><td>Implemented (optional).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Model call token usage</td><td>Cumulative usage counters</td><td>Implemented in-process; drives the token budget.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Run duration and tool-call counts</td><td>Session index</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Correction count per run</td><td>Session record</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nodes per run and best metric</td><td>Run listing API</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Cost per run</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; token usage is counted but never converted to cost, and is not persisted per run.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Success rate over time</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no aggregation exists. Outcomes are recorded per run but never rolled up.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Guardrail flag rate</td><td>Per-session findings</td><td>Partial &mdash; per-session only, never aggregated.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>15.2 Logs</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Log</th><th style="width:26%">Location</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
<tr><td>Session trace</td><td><span class="m">sessions/&lt;uuid&gt;/trace.json</span></td><td>Every step with timestamp and payload; tool-call counts; outcome.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Session summary</td><td><span class="m">sessions/&lt;uuid&gt;/summary.md</span></td><td>Human-readable replay.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Session index</td><td><span class="m">sessions/index.json</span></td><td>Last 100 runs.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Search journal</td><td><span class="m">runs/&lt;id&gt;/journal.json</span></td><td>Full solution tree.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Search report</td><td><span class="m">runs/&lt;id&gt;/report.md</span></td><td>Tree, metric history, failure analysis.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Application log</td><td>stdout</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; there is no structured application logger. Diagnostics are printed to standard output at varying formats. A proper logging configuration with levels is missing.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>15.3 Alerts</h2>
<p><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; no alerting exists. Nothing notifies anyone when a run fails, when a token budget is exhausted, when the correction abort triggers, when a guardrail flags content, or when the model endpoint is unreachable. For a local developer tool this is defensible because a human is watching. It becomes a gap the moment runs are triggered by a schedule or by an MCP client.</span></p>

<h2>15.4 Dashboards</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:30%">View</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>Live run feed</td><td>Implemented (same-process runs only).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Session history and detail</td><td>Implemented. <span class="todo">Note the timestamp-key defect (&sect;7.2.3).</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Search run tree and report</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Playbook</td><td>Implemented.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operational dashboard (success rate, cost, latency trends)</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; does not exist.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>15.5 Tracing</h2>
<p>OpenTelemetry spans wrap the model call and every tool call, carrying tool name, duration and success. Tracing is deliberately separate from the session trace mechanism: session traces are for human replay, spans are for observability tooling, and forcing one format to serve both would compromise both. Tracing is opt-in and becomes a complete no-op when the SDK is absent. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; spans are not correlated with a session identifier, so a trace in a collector cannot be joined to its session record. Add the session id as a span attribute.</span></p>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>16. Testing Requirements</h1>

<h2>16.1 Current position</h2>
<p>The delivered suite is 58 tests across 9 modules, runnable two ways: a zero-dependency runner requiring no third-party test framework and no network, or the standard framework if installed. The suite is deterministic and CI-ready.</p>

<p class="note"><b>An important distinction, and a correction.</b> The design documentation describes many behaviours as "verified directly" &mdash; and they were, by the author, manually, at the time they were built. That is not the same as being covered by the automated suite, and the two must not be conflated in a specification. The tables below report only what the <b>committed test suite</b> exercises. Behaviour that was manually verified during development but has no regression test is listed separately in &sect;16.8, because it will not be re-checked on any future change. That gap is the most actionable finding in this section.</p>

<h2>16.2 Unit testing &mdash; what the committed suite covers</h2>
<p class="legend">Nine test modules exist: <span class="m">test_llm_layer</span>, <span class="m">test_execution</span>, <span class="m">test_journal</span>, <span class="m">test_search_agent</span>, <span class="m">test_static_check</span>, <span class="m">test_knowledge</span>, <span class="m">test_doom_loop</span>, <span class="m">test_e2e_search</span>, <span class="m">test_parallel_resume</span>.</p>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:34%">Area</th><th style="width:16%">Covered</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Endpoint routing and client selection</td><td>Yes</td><td>Including the mock-client escape hatch.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Message and tool format conversion</td><td>Yes</td><td>Internal format to OpenAI function calling.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retry policy</td><td>Yes</td><td>Retryable versus non-retryable classification.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Subprocess execution and timeouts</td><td>Yes</td><td>Including partial-output preservation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Journal tree operations and persistence</td><td>Yes</td><td>Including round-trip serialisation.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Search policy transitions</td><td>Yes</td><td>Including parallel reservations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Static gate</td><td>Yes</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Knowledge store and reflection</td><td>Yes</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr><td>Doom-loop detection</td><td>Yes</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Review parsing</td><td>Yes</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-correction classification</td><td><b>No</b></td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; there is no test module for <span class="m">self_correction.py</span>. None of the seven error kinds has a regression test, despite FR-005 being a High-priority requirement.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Session memory and persistence</td><td><b>No</b></td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; there is no test module for <span class="m">memory.py</span>. The subscriber-isolation and index-capping behaviours in FR-006 are untested.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Tool registry filtering and path guard</td><td><b>No</b></td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; there is no test module for <span class="m">tools.py</span>. Path-traversal rejection (DC-07, SEC-006) has no regression test.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Context compaction</td><td><b>No</b></td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; implemented in the agent loop, not in the doom-loop module, and not covered.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Docker execution backend</td><td>No</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; requires a daemon; not exercised by the offline suite. Container recycling after timeout is unverified in CI &mdash; the isolation path most relevant to security is the least tested.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>16.3 Integration testing</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:34%">Scenario</th><th style="width:16%">In the suite</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>External MCP server connect / call / disconnect / reconnect</td><td><b>No</b></td><td>Manually verified during development against a real purpose-built server as a genuine subprocess (see &sect;16.8). No committed test.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Dynamic tool visibility per role</td><td><b>No</b></td><td>Manually verified during development. No committed test.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dashboard live streaming</td><td><b>No</b></td><td>Manually verified end-to-end during development with a real server and WebSocket client. No committed test.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Orchestrator routing</td><td><b>No</b></td><td>Manually verified during development using a scripted stand-in for the agent loop. No committed test.</td></tr>
<tr><td>MCP server tools end-to-end</td><td>No</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; not covered and not manually verified.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>16.4 System testing</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:34%">Scenario</th><th style="width:16%">Covered</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Full offline search, sequential</td><td>Yes</td><td>Synthetic data, mock model, <b>real execution</b>.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Full offline search, parallel</td><td>Yes</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr><td>Resume</td><td>Yes</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Token budget enforcement</td><td>Yes</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full run against the live endpoint</td><td>No</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; every automated test uses the mock client. The system has never been verified end-to-end against the real model in an automated way. This is the largest testing gap.</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>16.5 Performance testing</h2>
<p><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; none has been performed. Required at minimum: (a) measure the parallel speed-up claim in NFR-005; (b) measure journal write cost at 100+ nodes; (c) measure full-text retrieval latency at 1,000+ archived runs; (d) establish the practical dataset size ceiling.</span></p>

<h2>16.6 Security testing</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:34%">Test</th><th style="width:16%">Covered</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Guardrail detection, both directions</td><td>Runtime harness only</td><td>Real patterns must flag; benign look-alikes must not. Both directions matter equally. <span class="todo">TODO &mdash; this is a runtime command, not a test in the suite, so it does not run in CI and cannot fail a build.</span></td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Path traversal rejection</td><td><b>No</b></td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; the guard is implemented but has no regression test. Add one, and extend it to the run-id path parameter in API-005.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Live injection resistance</td><td>No</td><td>The benchmark tests the static detector, not whether a real run resists a live attempt. That is a live-system question this harness cannot answer.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Sandbox escape</td><td>No</td><td><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; never attempted. Note that under the subprocess fallback there is no sandbox to escape.</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependency vulnerability scan</td><td>No</td><td><span class="todo">TODO</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>16.7 Acceptance testing and known gaps</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:34%">Gap</th><th>Consequence</th></tr>
<tr><td>No automated test against the live model endpoint.</td><td>Prompt quality, tool-calling reliability and real-world behaviour are unevidenced by the suite.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Role prompt quality never evaluated.</td><td>Multi-agent routing is proven; multi-agent <i>usefulness</i> is not (FR-024).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vision and audio indexing never run end-to-end.</td><td>FR-027 paths are unproven in any environment.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Fine-tuning training and merge never run.</td><td>FR-028's expensive question &mdash; does training converge &mdash; is unanswered.</td></tr>
<tr><td>No benchmark run against MLE-bench.</td><td>The project's headline competitive claim has no supporting measurement.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Docker backend untested in CI.</td><td>The isolation path most relevant to security is the one least verified.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Four modules implementing High-priority requirements have no test module at all: self-correction, session memory, tool registry, orchestrator.</td><td>Regressions in these will not be caught. See &sect;16.8.</td></tr>
</table>
<p><span class="todo">TODO &mdash; formal user acceptance criteria and a sign-off process have not been defined.</span></p>

<h2>16.8 Manually verified but not regression-tested</h2>
<p>Each behaviour below is documented in the design material as having been verified directly during development, and there is no reason to doubt that it was. But none has a committed test, so none will be re-checked when the code around it changes. Several of these are exactly the subtle, hard-won fixes the design documentation is most proud of &mdash; which makes them the ones most worth protecting.</p>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:38%">Behaviour</th><th style="width:14%">Requirement</th><th>Why it needs a test</th></tr>
<tr><td>Self-correction runs before the guardrail scan.</td><td>FR-001 AC5, BR-019</td><td>This exact ordering was a real defect. Reversing it silently disables error detection &mdash; a failure mode with no visible symptom.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Seven error kinds classify correctly.</td><td>FR-005</td><td>The classification order is first-match-wins; reordering it changes behaviour silently.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dynamic MCP tool visibility is granted only to trusted roles.</td><td>FR-002 AC3, BR-012</td><td>A regression here silently widens what an untrusted role can do.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>MCP connect / call / disconnect / reconnect against a real server.</td><td>FR-025</td><td>The one-task-per-connection design exists to avoid a specific async cancel-scope failure. A refactor could reintroduce it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A raising subscriber does not crash a run.</td><td>FR-006 AC5, BR-027</td><td>Protects the agent from its own observers.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Session index capping and upsert semantics.</td><td>FR-006 AC2</td><td>Unbounded growth would be silent.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Orchestrator routing, including ambiguous-verdict-as-rejection.</td><td>FR-024, BR-008</td><td>Fail-safe behaviour that is easy to "simplify" into fail-open.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Dashboard live streaming end-to-end.</td><td>FR-032 AC2</td><td>Crosses a thread/event-loop boundary &mdash; the kind of code that breaks quietly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Path traversal rejection.</td><td>DC-07, SEC-006</td><td>A security control with no test is a security control you are guessing about.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Generated deployment service compiles.</td><td>FR-023 AC1, BR-024</td><td>The original defect was invisible on inspection and appeared only on compilation.</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Recommendation.</b> Adding test modules for <span class="m">self_correction.py</span>, <span class="m">memory.py</span>, <span class="m">tools.py</span> and <span class="m">orchestrator.py</span> would close most of this table. All four are pure-logic modules with no external dependencies, so they fit the existing offline, zero-dependency suite without any new infrastructure. This is the highest value-per-hour work available on the project right now.</p>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>17. Risks</h1>

<p class="legend">Severity reflects impact &times; likelihood. Risks are ordered by severity, highest first.</p>

<h2>17.1 Technical risks</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:9%">ID</th><th style="width:26%">Risk</th><th style="width:11%">Severity</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-01</td><td>Single-endpoint dependency. All functionality stops if the model endpoint is unavailable, and the endpoint currently in use is a test deployment.</td><td>High</td><td>Designate a production endpoint with an availability commitment. Retain the ability to repoint by configuration (already present). Consider a documented secondary endpoint.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-02</td><td>Silent loss of isolation. When Docker is unavailable the system falls back to subprocess execution automatically and without notice, running agent-generated code directly on the host.</td><td>High</td><td>Announce the fallback prominently at startup. Provide a strict mode that refuses to run without container isolation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-03</td><td>Unauthenticated remote code execution. The dashboard's run endpoint executes arbitrary agent tasks with no authentication; a single host flag exposes it to the network.</td><td>High</td><td>Add authentication before any non-loopback bind is permitted. Refuse to start on a non-loopback host unless authentication is configured.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-04</td><td>Unverified against the live model. Every automated test uses a mock. Real-world reliability is unevidenced.</td><td>High</td><td>Add a small, budgeted live smoke suite run on a schedule rather than on every commit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-05</td><td>Unmanaged disk growth. Run directories copy input data and are never pruned.</td><td>Medium</td><td>Define a retention policy; make data symlinking rather than copying the default where safe.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-06</td><td>Archive duplication degrades learning. Missing uniqueness on the run archive means repeated indexing skews similarity retrieval over time.</td><td>Medium</td><td>Add a uniqueness constraint or convert to an upsert. Low effort, and it protects the headline differentiator.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-07</td><td>Unbounded thread creation in the MCP server. One thread per submission with no cap.</td><td>Medium</td><td>Introduce a bounded pool and a submission queue.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-08</td><td>Missing HTTP request timeout. A hung model request could consume all five retry attempts.</td><td>Medium</td><td>Set an explicit client timeout. Low effort.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-09</td><td>Guardrails oversold. A small illustrative pattern list could be mistaken for a security control.</td><td>Medium</td><td>State the limitation wherever the capability is described. Do not present it as protection.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-10</td><td>Structural live-streaming limitation. Cross-process runs cannot stream live without a broker, which DC-04 forbids.</td><td>Low</td><td>Accept and surface it in the interface, or revisit DC-04 deliberately.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-11</td><td>Unproven optional capabilities. Vision indexing, audio indexing and fine-tuning have never run end-to-end.</td><td>Low</td><td>Either verify them in the target environment or mark them explicitly experimental.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-18</td><td>Four modules implementing High-priority requirements have no test module at all &mdash; self-correction, session memory, tool registry and orchestrator. Several subtle fixes the design documentation records as "verified directly" have no regression protection.</td><td>High</td><td>Add offline test modules for all four. They are pure-logic modules that fit the existing zero-dependency suite with no new infrastructure (&sect;16.8).</td></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-19</td><td>Unauthenticated switch into mock mode. Posting <span class="m">{"model": "mock"}</span> to the run endpoint silently substitutes the offline scripted client, so a run can appear to succeed with no real model involvement.</td><td>Medium</td><td>Reject mock specs on the HTTP surface, or gate them behind an explicit development flag.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-20</td><td>Silent task-type misclassification. A non-numeric target with high cardinality is classified as regression rather than rejected, producing a meaningless model instead of an error.</td><td>Medium</td><td>Make the fallback explicit and reject unclassifiable targets (FR-021).</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>17.2 Business risks</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:9%">ID</th><th style="width:26%">Risk</th><th style="width:11%">Severity</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-12</td><td>The headline competitive claim ("beat HeyNeo") has no measurement behind it and no agreed target.</td><td>High</td><td>Agree an acceptance target (&sect;2.2.3) and run the benchmark before the claim is made externally.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-13</td><td>Data leaves the organisation. Task descriptions and data previews are sent to a third-party endpoint with no residency guarantee, redaction or opt-out.</td><td>High</td><td>Confirm what data will realistically be processed. If it is customer or personal data, this blocks production until resolved.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-14</td><td>Undefined compliance obligations (&sect;5.8).</td><td>Medium</td><td>Get a decision from the manager on the regulatory context. This is not something engineering can infer.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-15</td><td>Model cost is uncontrolled in aggregate. Per-run budgets exist; there is no organisational cap or cost reporting.</td><td>Medium</td><td>Persist token usage per run and report on it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>RISK-16</td><td>Key-person dependency. The design rationale is captured in detail, but the system is complex and currently maintained by one author.</td><td>Medium</td><td>The existing documentation is unusually good; use it for a structured handover session with a second engineer.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>RISK-17</td><td>Third-party licence exposure across a large optional dependency set.</td><td>Low</td><td>Run a licence audit.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>

<h1>18. Future Enhancements</h1>

<h2>18.1 Improvements already identified in the design material</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:10%">ID</th><th style="width:34%">Enhancement</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>FE-01</td><td>Persisted MCP server configuration.</td><td>Removes manual reconnection after every restart.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>FE-02</td><td>SSE and HTTP transports for the MCP client.</td><td>A change inside transport selection, not a redesign of the bridge.</td></tr>
<tr><td>FE-03</td><td>Model registry versioning.</td><td>Retraining under the same name currently overwrites the artifact with no history.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>FE-04</td><td>Improved role prompts.</td><td>Requires live evaluation first (FR-024).</td></tr>
<tr><td>FE-05</td><td>Cross-process live streaming via a shared broker.</td><td>Explicitly deferred under DC-04. If it becomes a real requirement, a pub/sub broker is the stated path forward, not a larger in-memory mechanism.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>FE-06</td><td>Fine-tuning the deployed model on archived winning runs.</td><td>Noted as the natural extension now that the platform runs its own open-weight model. This would close the learning loop from playbook text into model weights.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>18.2 Improvements identified while writing this specification</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:10%">ID</th><th style="width:34%">Enhancement</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>FE-07</td><td>Authentication and authorization across all interfaces.</td><td>Prerequisite for any use beyond a single trusted machine. Currently the largest gap.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>FE-08</td><td>Proper HTTP status codes and an API version segment.</td><td>Today every failure is a 200 and there is no versioning; both defeat normal client behaviour.</td></tr>
<tr><td>FE-09</td><td>Asynchronous submit-and-poll for the HTTP run endpoint.</td><td>Aligns it with the MCP surface and stops clients hitting their own timeouts on long runs.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>FE-10</td><td>Per-run cost and token persistence, plus aggregate reporting.</td><td>Turns the existing token counting into something a manager can act on.</td></tr>
<tr><td>FE-11</td><td>Data retention policies for runs, sessions and the archive.</td><td>Disk growth is currently unmanaged and fastest in the largest store.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>FE-12</td><td>Session id as a span attribute.</td><td>Makes traces joinable to session records; small change, large diagnostic value.</td></tr>
<tr><td>FE-13</td><td>Structured application logging with levels.</td><td>Replaces ad-hoc printing to standard output.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>FE-14</td><td>A strict isolation mode that refuses to run without a container.</td><td>Closes RISK-02.</td></tr>
<tr><td>FE-15</td><td>Prompt/response caching.</td><td>Repeated drafts currently re-bill identical prompts.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>18.3 Possible integrations</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:30%">Integration</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Experiment tracking (MLflow, Weights &amp; Biases)</td><td>Replaces the bespoke run listing with tooling teams already use.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Model registry</td><td>Addresses FE-03 properly rather than in-memory.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Object storage for run artifacts</td><td>Addresses retention and makes runs shareable.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Issue trackers and chat platforms over MCP</td><td>Swarn already consumes MCP servers; this needs no new mechanism.</td></tr>
<tr><td>An observability backend</td><td>Spans are already emitted; only a collector is missing.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>CI integration</td><td>The CLI already returns meaningful exit codes specifically to support this.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>18.4 Scalability roadmap</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:14%">Stage</th><th style="width:26%">Target</th><th>Required change</th></tr>
<tr><td>Now</td><td>Single user, single process.</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Next</td><td>Single user, multiple concurrent runs.</td><td>Externalise the workspace and dataset registries per run; cap thread creation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Then</td><td>Small team, shared instance.</td><td>Authentication and authorization (FE-07); per-user workspace isolation; retention policy; TLS.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Later</td><td>Multi-replica hosted service.</td><td>Requires revisiting DC-04 (broker), externalising all singleton state, and a real deployment model. This is a re-architecture, not a scaling exercise &mdash; it should be treated as a separate project with its own specification.</td></tr>
</table>

