<h1>20. Open Questions</h1>

<p>These require a decision from the manager or product owner. They cannot be resolved by reading the code, and each one changes what this document should say.</p>

<table>
<tr><th style="width:9%">ID</th><th style="width:46%">Question</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>OQ-01</td><td>Will Swarn ever run anywhere other than a single trusted developer machine?</td><td>This single answer determines whether &sect;11 is a minor gap or a blocking programme of work.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>OQ-02</td><td>What is the acceptance target? Is "beat HeyNeo's 34.2% on MLE-bench" the actual bar, or is it directional?</td><td>Without this the project has no definition of done (RISK-12).</td></tr>
<tr><td>OQ-03</td><td>What data will realistically be processed &mdash; synthetic, internal, or customer data?</td><td>Drives compliance (&sect;5.8), residency (RISK-13) and retention (&sect;7.7).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>OQ-04</td><td>Which endpoint is the production model, and who operates it?</td><td>The current default is a hard-coded third-party test deployment (SEC-011, RISK-01).</td></tr>
<tr><td>OQ-05</td><td>Is single-user operation acceptable for v1, or is a shared team instance expected?</td><td>Shared use means re-architecture, not configuration (NFR-010, &sect;18.4).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>OQ-06</td><td>What is the expected dataset size range?</td><td>The in-memory registry has a hard ceiling at host memory (AS-04).</td></tr>
<tr><td>OQ-07</td><td>Should isolation be mandatory, refusing to run without Docker?</td><td>Currently the fallback is silent and removes isolation entirely (RISK-02).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>OQ-08</td><td>Is a live-model test suite affordable, and at what token budget and cadence?</td><td>This is the largest verification gap (RISK-04).</td></tr>
<tr><td>OQ-09</td><td>Who owns operations &mdash; retention, backups, endpoint availability?</td><td>Currently nobody, because there is no deployment model.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>OQ-10</td><td>Should FR-027 (vision/audio) and FR-028 (fine-tuning) remain in v1 scope given they have never been run end-to-end?</td><td>Marking them experimental is more honest than listing them as delivered features.</td></tr>
<tr><td>OQ-11</td><td>Is a documentation-only mobile/responsive dashboard acceptable, or will managers review runs from a phone?</td><td>Determines whether &sect;10.6 is a real requirement.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>OQ-12</td><td>Does the unparsed design document (&sect;1.4) contain requirements not present in the READMEs or code?</td><td>If so, this specification is incomplete until they are merged in.</td></tr>
</table>

<h1>21. Missing Information</h1>

<p>Information this specification needed and could not obtain. Each of these is currently marked TODO in the body.</p>

<table>
<tr><th style="width:30%">Area</th><th>What is missing</th></tr>
<tr><td>Service levels</td><td>Every response time, throughput and availability target. None exist in the design material; every number in &sect;13 is an assumption or a coded constant.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Capacity</td><td>Expected concurrent users, dataset sizes, run volumes, storage sizing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security</td><td>The entire authentication and authorization model, TLS position, secret handling policy, and audit attribution.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Compliance</td><td>Regulatory context, data classification, retention obligations, licence audit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deployment</td><td>Production environment definition, CI/CD pipeline, release and versioning process.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Operations</td><td>Backup, recovery, alerting, on-call ownership, corrupt-journal recovery.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accessibility &amp; localization</td><td>Any stated requirement at all.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Diagrams</td><td>Sequence, ER and API diagrams (&sect;19.4&ndash;19.6). The architecture diagram exists but is not embedded.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Benchmark evidence</td><td>Any measured result supporting the competitive claim or the parallel speed-up claim.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Design document contents</td><td><span class="m">Swarn_Design_and_Engineering_Document.docx</span> could not be parsed in the authoring environment (&sect;1.4).</td></tr>
</table>

<h1>22. Suggestions for Improvement</h1>

<p>Ordered by value relative to effort. The first four are small, concrete changes that close disproportionate risk.</p>

<h2>22.1 Quick wins &mdash; low effort, high value</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:9%">ID</th><th style="width:38%">Suggestion</th><th>Closes</th></tr>
<tr><td>SG-01</td><td>Set an explicit HTTP request timeout on the model client.</td><td>NFR-050, RISK-08. A few lines.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SG-02</td><td>Add a uniqueness constraint on the run archive, or convert insertion to an upsert.</td><td>DR-06, RISK-06. Protects the headline differentiator from silently degrading.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SG-03</td><td>Unify the step timestamp key across persistence and the live stream.</td><td>A visible defect in the session detail view (&sect;7.2.3).</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SG-04</td><td>Print a prominent warning when the execution backend falls back to subprocess.</td><td>RISK-02. Turns a silent loss of isolation into an informed choice.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SG-05</td><td>Correct three documentation inaccuracies: the CLI docstring's missing commands, the MCP model-parameter description, and the knowledge module's stated paths.</td><td>Small, but these are exactly the things a new engineer trusts and is misled by.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SG-06</td><td>Add the two tracing variables to the example environment file.</td><td>Makes an existing capability discoverable.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>22.2 Structural &mdash; do before any shared use</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:9%">ID</th><th style="width:38%">Suggestion</th><th>Closes</th></tr>
<tr><td>SG-07</td><td>Add authentication to the dashboard, and refuse to start on a non-loopback host without it.</td><td>SEC-001, RISK-03. This is the single highest-value change in the list.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SG-08</td><td>Return proper HTTP status codes and add an API version segment.</td><td>API-X2, API-X3.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SG-09</td><td>Bound thread creation in the MCP server with a pool and a queue.</td><td>RISK-07.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SG-10</td><td>Define and implement retention for runs, sessions and the archive; make data symlinking the default where safe.</td><td>&sect;7.7, RISK-05.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SG-11</td><td>Persist per-run token usage and add a cost view.</td><td>FE-10, RISK-15. Directly useful to management.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>22.3 Verification &mdash; converts claims into evidence</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:9%">ID</th><th style="width:38%">Suggestion</th><th>Closes</th></tr>
<tr><td>SG-12</td><td>Add a small, budgeted live-endpoint smoke suite on a schedule rather than per commit.</td><td>RISK-04 &mdash; the largest verification gap.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SG-13</td><td>Run the benchmark and record the number.</td><td>RISK-12. Until this exists, the competitive claim is a design intention.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SG-14</td><td>Measure the parallel speed-up.</td><td>NFR-005. The claim is currently unverified.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SG-15</td><td>Wire the existing test suite to a CI pipeline.</td><td>&sect;14.5. The suite was designed for this; it simply is not connected.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SG-16</td><td>Either verify the vision, audio and fine-tuning paths in the target environment, or relabel them experimental.</td><td>RISK-11, OQ-10.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SG-17</td><td>Add offline test modules for <span class="m">self_correction.py</span>, <span class="m">memory.py</span>, <span class="m">tools.py</span> and <span class="m">orchestrator.py</span>.</td><td>RISK-18, &sect;16.8. <b>The highest value-per-hour work available on the project.</b> All four are pure-logic modules that fit the existing suite with no new infrastructure, and they protect the fixes the design documentation is most proud of.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SG-18</td><td>Reject mock model specs on the HTTP surface.</td><td>RISK-19. A few lines.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SG-19</td><td>Make the task-type fallback explicit and reject unclassifiable targets.</td><td>RISK-20, FR-021.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>22.4 A note on the documentation itself</h2>
<p>The quality of the existing design documentation is unusually high, and this is worth saying plainly to a reviewer. It records not only what was built but <i>why</i>, including several real defects, the reasoning that produced each fix, and the generalisable lesson from each. That is rare, and it is the reason this specification could be reverse-engineered with as few gaps as it has.</p>
<p>Two things would strengthen it further. First, separate the "what it does now" documentation from the "how it evolved" narrative &mdash; the phase history is valuable but it makes the current behaviour harder to read off. Second, keep the known-limitations list as a living document; it is the most useful section for a reviewer and the easiest to let go stale.</p>

<hr style="margin-top:30pt; border:none; border-top:1pt solid #ccc;">
<p style="font-size:9pt; color:#666; margin-top:8pt;"><i>End of document. This specification was derived from the delivered design documentation and source tree. No business logic has been invented; every gap is marked TODO, and every suggested value is marked as an assumption.</i></p>

</body>
